{"id":"AO-CARDIOLOGY-Q9","spec":"ao-cardiology","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Which of the following is true about Eisenmenger syndrome?","o":["Usually a primary congenital cyanotic heart disease.","Considered a high indication for surgery in a baby with congenital heart disease.","Represents a serious complication of non-cyanotic heart disease.","Pulmonary hypertension is not always present."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Eisenmenger syndrome is advanced pulmonary vascular disease caused by a longstanding systemic-to-pulmonary shunt.","more":"Explanation:\nEisenmenger syndrome is advanced pulmonary vascular disease caused by a longstanding systemic-to-pulmonary shunt. Progressive pulmonary vascular resistance eventually approaches or exceeds systemic resistance, reversing the shunt and causing cyanosis. It is therefore a serious late complication of initially acyanotic lesions such as large VSD, PDA or ASD, not a primary cyanotic congenital lesion. At this stage, closing the defect may be dangerous and is not a routine “indication for surgery.”\n\nOptions analysis:\na. Incorrect: Eisenmenger physiology is acquired after pulmonary vascular disease develops, not a primary cyanotic malformation.\nb. Incorrect: established irreversible pulmonary vascular disease is generally a contraindication to defect closure.\nc. Correct: it is a serious complication of an untreated non-cyanotic left-to-right shunt.\nd. Incorrect: pulmonary hypertension is fundamental to the syndrome.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nEisenmenger syndrome is advanced pulmonary vascular disease caused by a longstanding systemic-to-pulmonary shunt.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Eisenmenger syndrome complicating an initially acyanotic shunt","aoSource":"Lymph Notes in Pediatrics · PDF p. 65 · printed p. 64 · answer PDF p. 65 · original Q9","aoSystem":"Cardiology","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q1-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"An 8-year-old male presents to the emergency department with decreased mental status. He has been drinking and urinating more frequently over the past several weeks. He was hard to wake up this morning and complained of abdominal pain. Physical examination reveals dry mucus membranes and cracked lips. His abdomen is mildly tender to palpation diffusely, but there is no re-bound or guarding. Expected findings don't include:","o":["A blood glucose level of 560 mg/dl.","A blood pH value of 7.18.","Clear chest x-ray.","Low serum osmolarity."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"The polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness suggest diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA).","more":"Explanation:\nThe polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness suggest diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). Hyperglycemia raises serum effective osmolality, while insulin deficiency and counter-regulatory hormones drive ketogenesis and metabolic acidosis. The immediate priority is restoration of circulation with isotonic crystalloid, with careful reassessment and protocol-based subsequent fluid/insulin/potassium therapy. Sodium bicarbonate is not routine therapy in pediatric DKA.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Glucose 560 mg/dL: compatible with DKA; marked hyperglycemia is common.\n- I-b. pH 7.18: compatible with metabolic acidosis and DKA.\n- I-c. Clear chest X-ray: may be present; it does not exclude DKA.\n- I-d. Low serum osmolality: correct exception; hyperglycemia usually increases effective osmolality.\n- II-a. IV normal saline: correct initial fluid choice in the traditional question framework; balanced isotonic crystalloid may also be used by current protocols.\n- II-b. IV sodium bicarbonate: not routine; may worsen risk of cerebral injury and is reserved for exceptional indications.\n- II-c. IV mannitol: not initial DKA therapy; reserved for suspected cerebral injury under an appropriate protocol.\n- II-d. IV potassium: replace only after assessment of serum potassium and urine output; not before initial circulation assessment.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDKA treatment in children is protocol-driven: isotonic fluid first, insulin after initial fluid therapy, potassium guided by labs/urine output, and no routine bicarbonate.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q1 — DKA: expected laboratory findings and first fluid","aoSource":"PDF p. 41 · Printed p. 40 · Original question number: Q1","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q1-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"N.B. DKA = hypertonic dehydration. Part II: The most immediate initial therapy is:","o":["IV normal saline.","IV sodium bicarbonate.","IV mannitol.","IV potassium."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness suggest diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA).","more":"Explanation:\nThe polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness suggest diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). Hyperglycemia raises serum effective osmolality, while insulin deficiency and counter-regulatory hormones drive ketogenesis and metabolic acidosis. The immediate priority is restoration of circulation with isotonic crystalloid, with careful reassessment and protocol-based subsequent fluid/insulin/potassium therapy. Sodium bicarbonate is not routine therapy in pediatric DKA.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Glucose 560 mg/dL: compatible with DKA; marked hyperglycemia is common.\n- I-b. pH 7.18: compatible with metabolic acidosis and DKA.\n- I-c. Clear chest X-ray: may be present; it does not exclude DKA.\n- I-d. Low serum osmolality: correct exception; hyperglycemia usually increases effective osmolality.\n- II-a. IV normal saline: correct initial fluid choice in the traditional question framework; balanced isotonic crystalloid may also be used by current protocols.\n- II-b. IV sodium bicarbonate: not routine; may worsen risk of cerebral injury and is reserved for exceptional indications.\n- II-c. IV mannitol: not initial DKA therapy; reserved for suspected cerebral injury under an appropriate protocol.\n- II-d. IV potassium: replace only after assessment of serum potassium and urine output; not before initial circulation assessment.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDKA treatment in children is protocol-driven: isotonic fluid first, insulin after initial fluid therapy, potassium guided by labs/urine output, and no routine bicarbonate.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q1 — DKA: expected laboratory findings and first fluid","aoSource":"PDF p. 41 · Printed p. 40 · Original question number: Q1","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q10","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"The most urgent management of hypovolemic shock is:","o":["Oxygen therapy.","Assisted ventilation.","IV fluids.","IV antibiotics."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Hypovolemic shock results from inadequate circulating volume and reduced tissue perfusion.","more":"Explanation:\nHypovolemic shock results from inadequate circulating volume and reduced tissue perfusion. Management follows ABCDE: support oxygenation/ventilation as required, obtain vascular/intraosseous access, give isotonic crystalloid boluses with reassessment, control hemorrhage or replace gastrointestinal losses, and monitor for fluid overload. Antibiotics are not routine unless infection is the cause.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Oxygen: may be required, but it does not restore circulating volume.\n- b. Assisted ventilation: used when respiratory failure or airway compromise exists, not the main universal treatment for hypovolemia.\n- c. IV fluids: correct among the choices; isotonic crystalloid is used with repeated reassessment. Hemorrhagic shock may require early blood products.\n- d. IV antibiotics: indicated for infection/sepsis, not uncomplicated volume loss.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypovolemic shock results from inadequate circulating volume and reduced tissue perfusion.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q10 — Hypovolemic shock: urgent management","aoSource":"PDF p. 43 · Printed p. 42 · Original question number: Q10","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q11","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Acute congestive heart failure is not common in:","o":["Fallot tetralogy.","Acute renal failure.","Ventricular septal defect (VSD).","Severe tachycardia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Large left-to-right shunts such as VSD can produce pulmonary overcirculation and congestive heart failure in infancy.","more":"Explanation:\nLarge left-to-right shunts such as VSD can produce pulmonary overcirculation and congestive heart failure in infancy. Acute renal failure can cause fluid overload; sustained severe tachycardia can impair cardiac function. Classic tetralogy of Fallot usually produces cyanosis and reduced pulmonary blood flow rather than typical congestive failure, although coexisting lesions or unusual physiology can alter this.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Tetralogy of Fallot: correct; CHF is not the usual presentation in classic TOF.\n- b. Acute renal failure: fluid retention can precipitate heart failure.\n- c. VSD: large defects commonly cause pulmonary overcirculation and infant CHF.\n- d. Severe tachycardia: sustained tachyarrhythmia can cause tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy/heart failure.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nLarge left-to-right shunts such as VSD can produce pulmonary overcirculation and congestive heart failure in infancy.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q11 — Acute congestive heart failure: uncommon association","aoSource":"PDF p. 43 · Printed p. 42 · Original question number: Q11","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q12","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"One of the following is correct about croup:","o":["It's mostly due to viral infection.","Cold air aggravates the condition.","Females are much more affected than males.","Cause soft musical sound."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Croup is acute laryngotracheitis with subglottic airway edema, most often due to parainfluenza viruses.","more":"Explanation:\nCroup is acute laryngotracheitis with subglottic airway edema, most often due to parainfluenza viruses. It causes a barking cough, hoarseness and inspiratory stridor. It is not a lower-airway musical wheeze; wheeze is more typical of intrathoracic lower-airway obstruction.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Mostly viral: correct.\n- b. Cold air aggravates the condition: not a defining rule; cool air may transiently ease symptoms in some children but is not standard treatment.\n- c. Females much more affected: incorrect; croup is more common in boys in many cohorts, but the difference is not the key diagnostic feature.\n- d. Soft musical sound: describes wheeze more than the harsh inspiratory stridor of croup.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCroup is acute laryngotracheitis with subglottic airway edema, most often due to parainfluenza viruses.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q12 — Croup: viral etiology","aoSource":"PDF p. 43 · Printed p. 42 · Original question number: Q12","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q13","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Common causes of stridor during include the following except:","o":["Laryngeal foreign body.","Acute spasmodic laryngitis.","Large adenoid.","Hypocalcemia tetany."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Stridor is a harsh, high-pitched sound from upper-airway narrowing.","more":"Explanation:\nStridor is a harsh, high-pitched sound from upper-airway narrowing. Causes include laryngeal foreign body, croup/spasmodic laryngitis, epiglottitis, laryngomalacia and laryngeal edema. Hypocalcemic tetany can cause laryngospasm; enlarged adenoids cause nasal obstruction and snoring/stertor rather than classic laryngeal stridor.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Laryngeal foreign body: can produce acute upper-airway obstruction and stridor.\n- b. Acute spasmodic laryngitis: a croup syndrome and a recognized cause of stridor.\n- c. Large adenoid: correct exception; generally causes nasal obstruction/stertor, not laryngeal stridor.\n- d. Hypocalcemia tetany: may cause laryngospasm and stridor.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nStridor is a harsh, high-pitched sound from upper-airway narrowing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q13 — Stridor: exception among possible causes","aoSource":"PDF p. 43 · Printed p. 42 · Original question number: Q13","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q14","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"In respiratory failure, all the following statements are true except:","o":["Can be caused by upper air way obstruction.","PaCO2 is always elevated in type 2 respiratory failure.","All hypoxic patients need endotracheal intubation & mechanical ventilation.","Acute metabolic acidosis is the typical in type 1 RF."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Respiratory failure may result from upper-airway obstruction.","more":"Explanation:\nRespiratory failure may result from upper-airway obstruction. Type 2 respiratory failure is defined by hypercapnia (raised PaCO2). Hypoxia alone does not mandate intubation: treatment depends on severity, work of breathing, mental status, response to oxygen/non-invasive support and the underlying cause. “Acute metabolic acidosis” is not the standard defining disturbance of type 1 failure.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True: severe upper-airway obstruction may cause respiratory failure.\n- b. True by definition of type 2 failure: PaCO2 is elevated; “always” can be misleading if a mixed sample or measurement problem exists, but raised PaCO2 defines the category.\n- c. False: not every hypoxic patient needs intubation; many respond to oxygen and treatment of the cause.\n- d. Also not generally true: metabolic acidosis is not the typical defining pattern of type 1 failure. This undermines the single-exception format.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRespiratory failure may result from upper-airway obstruction.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q14 — Respiratory failure: multiple potentially false statements","aoSource":"PDF p. 43 · Printed p. 42 · Original question number: Q14","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q15","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"In which grade of respiratory distress cyanosis appears:","o":["Grade l.","Grade 2.","Grade 3.","Grade 4."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Central cyanosis suggests significant desaturation and is a serious sign of respiratory compromise.","more":"Explanation:\nCentral cyanosis suggests significant desaturation and is a serious sign of respiratory compromise. However, the exact “grade 4” label is not universal. The examination item needs the original lecture’s severity-grading table to verify the numerical grade. Clinically, do not wait for cyanosis before escalating care.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Grade 1: cannot be assessed without the specific grading scale.\n- b. Grade 2: cannot be assessed without the specific grading scale.\n- c. Grade 3: cannot be assessed without the specific grading scale.\n- d. Grade 4: source answer, but it is not independently verifiable without the scale definition.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCentral cyanosis suggests significant desaturation and is a serious sign of respiratory compromise.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q15 — Respiratory distress grade at which cyanosis appears","aoSource":"PDF p. 43 · Printed p. 42 · Original question number: Q15","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"","aoAltSource":"PDF p. 57 · Printed p. 56 · Original question number: Q23"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q16","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"In respiratory failure, which of the following is not correct:","o":["Can be caused by bronchial asthma.","Fatigue of respiratory muscle can lead to type 2 RF.","All patients should be mechanically ventilated.","Acute respiratory acidosis is typical finding in type 2 RF."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Severe asthma can cause respiratory failure, and respiratory-muscle fatigue with alveolar hypoventilation can lead to hypercapnia (type 2 failure).","more":"Explanation:\nSevere asthma can cause respiratory failure, and respiratory-muscle fatigue with alveolar hypoventilation can lead to hypercapnia (type 2 failure). Acute type 2 failure commonly causes respiratory acidosis. Management is individualized; oxygen, bronchodilators, non-invasive support or invasive ventilation are chosen according to clinical severity.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True: severe asthma can cause respiratory failure.\n- b. True: muscle fatigue leads to hypoventilation and CO2 retention.\n- c. Correct “not correct” option: not every patient needs mechanical ventilation.\n- d. True: acute CO2 retention produces respiratory acidosis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSevere asthma can cause respiratory failure, and respiratory-muscle fatigue with alveolar hypoventilation can lead to hypercapnia (type 2 failure).","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q16 — Respiratory failure: mechanical ventilation is not universal","aoSource":"PDF p. 44 · Printed p. 43 · Original question number: Q16","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q17","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"The O2 analyzer is a device used for:","o":["Knowing the FiO2 in a head box.","Knowing the FiO2 of gas coming from ventilator.","Knowing the patient's PaCO2.","Knowing the patients SaO2."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"An oxygen analyzer measures oxygen concentration (FiO2) in a delivered gas mixture.","more":"Explanation:\nAn oxygen analyzer measures oxygen concentration (FiO2) in a delivered gas mixture. It does not measure PaCO2 or arterial oxygen saturation directly. It may be used in oxygen hoods/head boxes and in ventilator circuits, depending on the device and monitoring setup. Therefore, the source’s intended answer a is understandable, but b may also be technically true.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. FiO2 in a head box: correct use.\n- b. FiO2 of gas coming from a ventilator: can also be measured by an oxygen analyzer, depending on circuit/device; this makes the single-best-answer question imprecise.\n- c. PaCO2: measured by blood gas analysis or monitored by capnography as end-tidal CO2, not by an oxygen analyzer.\n- d. SaO2: measured by pulse oximetry (SpO2 is the non-invasive estimate), not an oxygen analyzer.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAn oxygen analyzer measures oxygen concentration (FiO2) in a delivered gas mixture.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q17 — Oxygen analyzer: measuring inspired oxygen concentration","aoSource":"PDF p. 44 · Printed p. 43 · Original question number: Q17","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q18","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Bag &mask ventilation is contraindicated in:","o":["Birth asphyxia.","Neonatal apnea.","Hyaline membrane disease.","Diaphragmatic hernia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"In congenital diaphragmatic hernia, abdominal viscera may occupy the thoracic cavity and compress the lungs.","more":"Explanation:\nIn congenital diaphragmatic hernia, abdominal viscera may occupy the thoracic cavity and compress the lungs. Positive-pressure mask ventilation can insufflate the stomach and bowel, worsening intrathoracic compression. Early endotracheal intubation and gastric decompression are generally preferred when significant respiratory support is needed.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Birth asphyxia: bag-mask ventilation is a key initial resuscitation method when indicated.\n- b. Neonatal apnea: positive-pressure ventilation is indicated if the newborn is apneic or gasping.\n- c. Hyaline membrane disease: respiratory support may include CPAP or ventilation; bag-mask is not universally contraindicated.\n- d. Diaphragmatic hernia: correct; avoid mask ventilation when possible because gastric insufflation worsens thoracic compression.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIn congenital diaphragmatic hernia, abdominal viscera may occupy the thoracic cavity and compress the lungs.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q18 — Bag-mask ventilation contraindication in congenital diaphragmatic hernia","aoSource":"PDF p. 44 · Printed p. 43 · Original question number: Q18","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q19","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Regarding hemodynamic parameters in different types of shock, all are true, except:","o":["Cardiac output is decreased in hypovolemic shock.","Central venous pressure is decreased in hypovolemic shock.","Cardiac output is decreased in cardiogenic shock.","Central venous pressure is decreased in cardiogenic shock."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Hypovolemic shock reduces preload, CVP and cardiac output; systemic vascular resistance rises compensatorily.","more":"Explanation:\nHypovolemic shock reduces preload, CVP and cardiac output; systemic vascular resistance rises compensatorily. Cardiogenic shock reduces cardiac output despite adequate or elevated filling pressures, so CVP is usually increased. Real patients may have mixed shock states and CVP is an imperfect indicator of fluid responsiveness.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True: reduced circulating volume lowers preload and cardiac output.\n- b. True: CVP is usually low in hypovolemia.\n- c. True: pump failure reduces cardiac output.\n- d. Correct exception: CVP is generally raised in cardiogenic shock, not decreased.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypovolemic shock reduces preload, CVP and cardiac output; systemic vascular resistance rises compensatorily.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q19 — Shock hemodynamics: central venous pressure in cardiogenic shock","aoSource":"PDF p. 44 · Printed p. 43 · Original question number: Q19","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q2","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"The most important drug that maybe life-saving in a child with anaphylaxis is:","o":["IM epinephrine (adrenaline).","IV corticosteroid.","IM antihistamine.","Oral corticosteroid."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Anaphylaxis is an acute, potentially fatal systemic hypersensitivity reaction involving airway, breathing and/or circulation.","more":"Explanation:\nAnaphylaxis is an acute, potentially fatal systemic hypersensitivity reaction involving airway, breathing and/or circulation. IM epinephrine into the anterolateral thigh is the first-line treatment because alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction reduces mucosal edema and hypotension, while beta effects support bronchodilation and cardiac output. Adjuncts do not replace epinephrine.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. IM epinephrine: correct first-line life-saving treatment.\n- b. IV corticosteroid: not first-line and has delayed onset; should never delay epinephrine.\n- c. IM antihistamine: may help cutaneous symptoms but does not treat airway edema or shock.\n- d. Oral corticosteroid: delayed and inappropriate as the initial life-saving intervention.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAnaphylaxis is an acute, potentially fatal systemic hypersensitivity reaction involving airway, breathing and/or circulation.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q2 — Anaphylaxis: life-saving first-line drug","aoSource":"PDF p. 41 · Printed p. 40 · Original question number: Q2","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q20","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"What is the most appropriate management of hypovolemic shock:","o":["Dopamine infusion.","Adrenaline.","Sodium bicarbonate. .IV fluids."],"ol":["A","B","C"],"k":0,"ex":"Hypovolemic shock requires rapid restoration of circulating volume with an isotonic crystalloid, repeated assessment of response and treatment of the underlying loss.","more":"Explanation:\nHypovolemic shock requires rapid restoration of circulating volume with an isotonic crystalloid, repeated assessment of response and treatment of the underlying loss. Vasoactive drugs are not first-line for untreated volume depletion, and bicarbonate does not correct the underlying cause.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Dopamine: not first-line before correcting intravascular volume; may be considered in selected refractory shock states.\n- b. Adrenaline: vasoactive support for selected refractory or specific shock states, not the initial treatment of simple hypovolemia.\n- c. Sodium bicarbonate: does not restore volume and is not routine shock therapy.\n- [Unlabelled OCR option] IV fluids: correct; source label is absent and is not invented.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypovolemic shock requires rapid restoration of circulating volume with an isotonic crystalloid, repeated assessment of response and treatment of the underlying loss.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q20 — Hypovolemic shock: appropriate treatment","aoSource":"PDF p. 44 · Printed p. 43 · Original question number: Q20","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"","kx":true},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q21","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"All the following are true, except:","o":["Pulse oximeter is a device that measures oxygen saturation.","Pulse oximetry is simple and non-invasive.","Oxygen saturation is considered normal when it is >85%.","Pulse oximeter can't measure hyper-oxygenation of the blood."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Pulse oximetry non-invasively estimates arterial hemoglobin oxygen saturation (SpO2).","more":"Explanation:\nPulse oximetry non-invasively estimates arterial hemoglobin oxygen saturation (SpO2). In most healthy children at sea level, normal saturation is typically in the mid-to-high 90s. The device is insensitive to the degree of hyperoxia at very high PaO2 because the oxygen dissociation curve plateaus; arterial blood gas is needed when precise oxygenation assessment is required.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True: pulse oximetry estimates oxygen saturation.\n- b. True: it is non-invasive and simple to apply.\n- c. Correct exception: >85% is not generally a normal target for a healthy child at sea level.\n- d. True: it cannot reliably quantify hyperoxemia at high PaO2.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPulse oximetry non-invasively estimates arterial hemoglobin oxygen saturation (SpO2).","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q21 — Pulse oximetry: normal saturation claim","aoSource":"PDF p. 44 · Printed p. 43 · Original question number: Q21","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q22","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pharmacology","q":"Which of the following drugs is an afterload reducing agent:","o":["Digoxin.","Captopril.","Furosemide (Lasix).","Epinephrine."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Afterload is the resistance against which the ventricle ejects.","more":"Explanation:\nAfterload is the resistance against which the ventricle ejects. Captopril inhibits angiotensin-converting enzyme, reducing angiotensin II and aldosterone effects and lowering systemic vascular resistance. Digoxin increases contractility and affects AV conduction; furosemide reduces volume/preload; epinephrine generally increases cardiac stimulation and vascular tone depending on dose/receptor effects.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Digoxin: positive inotrope and AV nodal agent; not a primary afterload reducer.\n- b. Captopril: correct; ACE inhibition lowers afterload.\n- c. Furosemide: diuretic that reduces congestion/preload.\n- d. Epinephrine: inotrope/vasopressor; not an afterload-reducing drug.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAfterload is the resistance against which the ventricle ejects.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q22 — Captopril as an afterload-reducing agent","aoSource":"PDF p. 44 · Printed p. 43 · Original question number: Q22","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q23","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Etiology","q":"Croup is caused by:","o":["Rhinovirus.","Adenovirus.","Parainfluenza virus.","Influenza virus."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Croup is a viral inflammation of the larynx and subglottic trachea.","more":"Explanation:\nCroup is a viral inflammation of the larynx and subglottic trachea. Parainfluenza viruses are the classic and most common cause, though RSV, influenza, adenovirus and other respiratory viruses may also cause croup-like illness.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Rhinovirus: can cause respiratory illness but is not the classic most common cause.\n- b. Adenovirus: can cause croup-like illness, less commonly.\n- c. Parainfluenza virus: correct classic cause.\n- d. Influenza virus: may cause croup but is not the classic most common cause.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCroup is a viral inflammation of the larynx and subglottic trachea.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q23 — Croup: most common viral cause","aoSource":"PDF p. 45 · Printed p. 44 · Original question number: Q23","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q24","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 15-month-old infant has a history of poor oral fluid intake, occasional vomiting, rapid breathing, and decreased urine output (< 1 ml/kg/hour). Examination reveals a heart rate of 150 beats per minute, blood pressure of 120/80 mmHg, and a respiratory rate of 60/minute. There are bilateral basal crepitations and the liver is palpable 4 cm below the right costal margin. What is the most appropriate next step:","o":["Boluses of normal saline 20 ml/kg IV.","Start digoxin IV (digitalizing dose).","Give a dose of IV furosemide.","Initiate IV antibiotics."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Basal crepitations and hepatomegaly in a tachypneic infant suggest pulmonary and systemic congestion due to heart failure.","more":"Explanation:\nBasal crepitations and hepatomegaly in a tachypneic infant suggest pulmonary and systemic congestion due to heart failure. An IV loop diuretic such as furosemide can reduce congestion. A fluid bolus could worsen pulmonary edema. The high BP reading should be repeated with an appropriate cuff and interpreted by age/size; acute management needs clinician assessment and echocardiographic evaluation as indicated.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Normal saline bolus: potentially harmful in a congested child unless there is evidence of intravascular depletion/shock.\n- b. IV digoxin: not the immediate universal first step; use depends on diagnosis, ventricular function and rhythm.\n- c. IV furosemide: correct among the listed choices for symptomatic fluid overload/congestion.\n- d. IV antibiotics: only if infection is suspected; no evidence in this stem makes it the best immediate step.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nBasal crepitations and hepatomegaly in a tachypneic infant suggest pulmonary and systemic congestion due to heart failure.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q24 — Infant with congestive heart failure: next management step","aoSource":"PDF p. 45 · Printed p. 44 · Original question number: Q24","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q25","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"In arterial blood gases analysis (ABG), all the following are true except:","o":["Normal PaO2 is 95-100 mmHg.","Normal PaCO2 is 35-40 mmHg.","Normal pH is 7.1-7.25.","Normal bicarbonate is 22-26 mEq/L."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Usual arterial pH is approximately 7.35–7.45; PaCO2 is often about 35–45 mmHg; bicarbonate approximately 22–26 mEq/L.","more":"Explanation:\nUsual arterial pH is approximately 7.35–7.45; PaCO2 is often about 35–45 mmHg; bicarbonate approximately 22–26 mEq/L. PaO2 in a healthy child at sea level is commonly around 80–100 mmHg and depends on age, altitude and inspired oxygen. A pH of 7.1–7.25 is abnormal acidemia.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. PaO2 95–100 mmHg: a narrow upper-normal interval, not a complete reference range; not the clearly false option.\n- b. PaCO2 35–40 mmHg: a narrow range within common normal values; reference ranges often extend to about 45 mmHg.\n- c. pH 7.1–7.25: correct exception; this is acidemia.\n- d. HCO3 22–26 mEq/L: within common reference intervals.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nUsual arterial pH is approximately 7.35–7.45; PaCO2 is often about 35–45 mmHg; bicarbonate approximately 22–26 mEq/L.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q25 — Normal arterial blood gas values","aoSource":"PDF p. 45 · Printed p. 44 · Original question number: Q25","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q26","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Which of the following is not a feature of right sided heart failure:","o":["Ascites.","Hepatomegaly.","Limb edema.","Pulmonary edema."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Right ventricular failure causes systemic venous congestion, producing hepatomegaly, ascites, peripheral edema and raised venous pressure.","more":"Explanation:\nRight ventricular failure causes systemic venous congestion, producing hepatomegaly, ascites, peripheral edema and raised venous pressure. Left ventricular failure increases pulmonary venous pressure and may cause pulmonary edema. Mixed biventricular failure can produce both patterns.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Ascites: typical systemic venous congestion feature.\n- b. Hepatomegaly: typical in pediatric right-sided/systemic congestion.\n- c. Limb edema: can occur with systemic venous congestion.\n- d. Pulmonary edema: correct “not typical” choice; classically associated with left-sided failure.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nRight ventricular failure causes systemic venous congestion, producing hepatomegaly, ascites, peripheral edema and raised venous pressure.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q26 — Right-sided heart failure: feature that is not typical","aoSource":"PDF p. 45 · Printed p. 44 · Original question number: Q26","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q27-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 10-year-old girl is involved in a motor car accident, sustaining multiple injuries to her head, arms, and abdomen. Her BP is 90/60 mmHg, and her pulse is 120/minute. She has breathing difficulty, cyanotic lips, and rigid abdomen. Part I: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Septic shock.","Cardiogenic shock.","Hypovolemic shock.","Obstructive shock."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The mechanism, rigid abdomen and shock signs suggest intra-abdominal hemorrhage with hypovolemic/hemorrhagic shock.","more":"Explanation:\nThe mechanism, rigid abdomen and shock signs suggest intra-abdominal hemorrhage with hypovolemic/hemorrhagic shock. Immediate trauma care includes airway and breathing support, hemorrhage control, large-bore IV/IO access, blood sampling/crossmatch, and resuscitation. Balanced crystalloid may be used initially, but blood products should be available early in significant traumatic hemorrhage. Ultrasound is part of evaluation but does not replace resuscitation.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Septic shock: less likely immediately after trauma without infection.\n- I-b. Cardiogenic shock: less likely without evidence of primary pump failure.\n- I-c. Hypovolemic shock: correct, likely hemorrhagic due to abdominal trauma.\n- I-d. Obstructive shock: possible after trauma (e.g., tension pneumothorax/tamponade) but the rigid abdomen points toward bleeding.\n- II-a. Ultrasound: useful diagnostically, but should not delay initial resuscitation.\n- II-b. Crystalloid: source answer; an isotonic crystalloid is a reasonable initial bridge, with early blood products and hemorrhage control when bleeding is suspected.\n- II-c. Inotropes: not first-line for untreated hemorrhagic volume loss.\n- II-d. Exploratory laparotomy: may be needed for intra-abdominal bleeding, but operative decision follows rapid trauma assessment/resuscitation; do not delay circulation support.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe mechanism, rigid abdomen and shock signs suggest intra-abdominal hemorrhage with hypovolemic/hemorrhagic shock.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q27 — Traumatic hypovolemic shock: initial resuscitation","aoSource":"PDF p. 45 · Printed p. 44 · Original question number: Q27","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q27-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 10-year-old girl is involved in a motor car accident, sustaining multiple injuries to her head, arms, and abdomen. Her BP is 90/60 mmHg, and her pulse is 120/minute. She has breathing difficulty, cyanotic lips, and rigid abdomen. Part II: The most appropriate next step in management is:","o":["Do ultrasound abdomen.","Give crystalloid solution.","Administer inotropic drugs.","Perform exploratory laparotomy."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The mechanism, rigid abdomen and shock signs suggest intra-abdominal hemorrhage with hypovolemic/hemorrhagic shock.","more":"Explanation:\nThe mechanism, rigid abdomen and shock signs suggest intra-abdominal hemorrhage with hypovolemic/hemorrhagic shock. Immediate trauma care includes airway and breathing support, hemorrhage control, large-bore IV/IO access, blood sampling/crossmatch, and resuscitation. Balanced crystalloid may be used initially, but blood products should be available early in significant traumatic hemorrhage. Ultrasound is part of evaluation but does not replace resuscitation.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Septic shock: less likely immediately after trauma without infection.\n- I-b. Cardiogenic shock: less likely without evidence of primary pump failure.\n- I-c. Hypovolemic shock: correct, likely hemorrhagic due to abdominal trauma.\n- I-d. Obstructive shock: possible after trauma (e.g., tension pneumothorax/tamponade) but the rigid abdomen points toward bleeding.\n- II-a. Ultrasound: useful diagnostically, but should not delay initial resuscitation.\n- II-b. Crystalloid: source answer; an isotonic crystalloid is a reasonable initial bridge, with early blood products and hemorrhage control when bleeding is suspected.\n- II-c. Inotropes: not first-line for untreated hemorrhagic volume loss.\n- II-d. Exploratory laparotomy: may be needed for intra-abdominal bleeding, but operative decision follows rapid trauma assessment/resuscitation; do not delay circulation support.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe mechanism, rigid abdomen and shock signs suggest intra-abdominal hemorrhage with hypovolemic/hemorrhagic shock.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q27 — Traumatic hypovolemic shock: initial resuscitation","aoSource":"PDF p. 45 · Printed p. 44 · Original question number: Q27","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q28","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"The management of diabetic ketoacidosis must include all except:","o":["IV fluids.","IV insulin infusion.","Slow IV infusion of sodium bicarbonate.","Continuous monitoring of blood sugar."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Pediatric DKA management involves careful isotonic fluid replacement, insulin infusion after initial fluids (not an insulin bolus), potassium replacement according to serum levels and urine output, and frequent glucose, electrolyte, ketone, acid-base and neurological monitoring.","more":"Explanation:\nPediatric DKA management involves careful isotonic fluid replacement, insulin infusion after initial fluids (not an insulin bolus), potassium replacement according to serum levels and urine output, and frequent glucose, electrolyte, ketone, acid-base and neurological monitoring. Sodium bicarbonate is avoided routinely because it offers no demonstrated benefit and may increase the risk of cerebral injury; exceptional indications require specialist discussion.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. IV fluids: essential to restore circulation and replace deficit cautiously.\n- b. IV insulin infusion: essential to stop ketogenesis, started after initial fluid therapy and according to protocol.\n- c. Sodium bicarbonate: correct exception; not routine DKA therapy.\n- d. Continuous/frequent blood glucose monitoring: essential to guide treatment and prevent hypoglycemia.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPediatric DKA management involves careful isotonic fluid replacement, insulin infusion after initial fluids (not an insulin bolus), potassium replacement according to serum levels and urine output, and frequent glucose, electrolyte, ketone, acid-base and neurological monitoring.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q28 — DKA treatment: bicarbonate exception","aoSource":"PDF p. 46 · Printed p. 45 · Original question number: Q28","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q29","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Diagnosis","q":"A 5-year-old male is brought to the emergency room with harsh barking cough. He has history of coryza, nasal congestion & sore throat. His symptoms have been present for about a week. Examination reveals T 38°C, HR 100, RR 22. He has mild respiratory distress, stridor, intercostal interactions & nasal flaring. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Diphtheria.","Croup.","Epiglottitis.","Angioedema."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Croup (acute viral laryngotracheitis) produces a barking cough, hoarseness and inspiratory stridor due to subglottic edema.","more":"Explanation:\nCroup (acute viral laryngotracheitis) produces a barking cough, hoarseness and inspiratory stridor due to subglottic edema. Epiglottitis classically causes toxic appearance, drooling, severe sore throat, dysphagia and tripod posture, often without the barking cough. Diphtheria may cause pseudomembrane and toxic illness; angioedema causes acute swelling and possible airway compromise.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Diphtheria: less likely; look for pseudomembrane, toxic illness and incomplete immunization.\n- b. Croup: correct; barking cough, stridor and coryza fit.\n- c. Epiglottitis: typically severe sore throat, drooling, dysphagia, tripod posture and toxic appearance.\n- d. Angioedema: acute mucosal swelling, often with urticaria/allergic features; not a typical prolonged barking-cough syndrome.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCroup (acute viral laryngotracheitis) produces a barking cough, hoarseness and inspiratory stridor due to subglottic edema.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q29 — Croup clinical vignette","aoSource":"PDF p. 46 · Printed p. 45 · Original question number: Q29","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q3","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"The child who can't be aroused by painful stimuli, but responds by moaning or withdrawal movements is described to be in which grade of coma:","o":["Grade l.","Grade 2.","Grade 3.","Grade 4."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The stem describes a child who cannot be awakened normally but still shows a motor or vocal response to painful stimulation.","more":"Explanation:\nThe stem describes a child who cannot be awakened normally but still shows a motor or vocal response to painful stimulation. In the older grading scheme used by this question bank, that is classified as grade 2. Modern clinical documentation should use the actual components of the Glasgow Coma Scale (eye, verbal and motor responses) and describe brainstem/respiratory status separately.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Grade 1: generally less severe impairment, with response to voice or simple stimulation in the older scheme.\n- b. Grade 2: source answer; moaning or withdrawal to pain is compatible with this level in the implied grading system.\n- c. Grade 3: deeper coma, commonly with absent purposeful response to pain but spontaneous breathing may persist in the older scheme.\n- d. Grade 4: profound coma with absent response and potentially compromised brainstem function, depending on the scheme.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAlways document GCS components and trend; a label such as “grade II coma” is less reproducible than the actual responses.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q3 — Level of coma: response to painful stimulus","aoSource":"PDF p. 41 · Printed p. 40 · Original question number: Q3","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q30","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Which of the following is not true about croup:","o":["Harsh inspiratory sound.","Steam inhalation improves the condition.","All patients should be hospitalized.","Acute viral laryngitis is a common cause."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Croup causes a harsh inspiratory stridor and is commonly due to acute viral laryngotracheitis.","more":"Explanation:\nCroup causes a harsh inspiratory stridor and is commonly due to acute viral laryngotracheitis. Admission depends on severity, stridor at rest, work of breathing, oxygenation and response to treatment. Mild cases can often be managed without admission. Steam inhalation is not recommended because evidence shows no benefit and there is a scald/burn risk.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Harsh inspiratory sound: true; stridor is characteristic.\n- b. Steam inhalation improves the condition: not supported; steam is not recommended.\n- c. All patients should be hospitalized: false; mild cases do not all require admission.\n- d. Acute viral laryngitis/laryngotracheitis: true; common cause of croup.\nBoth b and c are false as written, so source answer c is not unique.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCroup causes a harsh inspiratory stridor and is commonly due to acute viral laryngotracheitis.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q30 — Croup: steam and hospitalization statements","aoSource":"PDF p. 46 · Printed p. 45 · Original question number: Q30","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q31","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"Regarding stridor, all are true except:","o":["It is a harsh inspiratory sound.","It is caused by partial obstruction of upper airways.","Grade 2 stridor is defined as stridor on exertion.","Patients with grade 3 stridor need hospital admission."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Stridor is a harsh, usually inspiratory sound caused by turbulent airflow through a narrowed upper airway.","more":"Explanation:\nStridor is a harsh, usually inspiratory sound caused by turbulent airflow through a narrowed upper airway. Severe or persistent stridor, especially at rest and with retractions, fatigue, cyanosis or altered consciousness, requires urgent assessment and often admission. A numerical grade label cannot be interpreted without the reference scale.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True: stridor is typically a harsh high-pitched inspiratory sound (may be biphasic with fixed obstruction).\n- b. True: it reflects turbulent airflow through a narrowed upper airway.\n- c. Not independently verifiable: grade 2 definitions vary; the course’s grading table is required.\n- d. Likely appropriate for significant/grade 3 stridor, but the grade system must be known to verify the statement.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nStridor is a harsh, usually inspiratory sound caused by turbulent airflow through a narrowed upper airway.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q31 — Stridor grading: definition of grade 2","aoSource":"PDF p. 46 · Printed p. 45 · Original question number: Q31","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q32-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 5-year-old boy developed severe sore throat, drippling of saliva. History of fever & increasing breathing difficulty over 8 hours. He looks toxic & unable to speak. Was sitting immobile upright with an open mouth to optimize the airway. Her airway was maintained with endotracheal tube: What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Acute bronchitis.","Acute epiglottitis.","Acute laryngitis.","Acute laryngotracheitis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Severe sore throat, drooling, toxic appearance, rapidly progressive respiratory distress, inability to speak and upright tripod posture are classic red flags for acute epiglottitis.","more":"Explanation:\nSevere sore throat, drooling, toxic appearance, rapidly progressive respiratory distress, inability to speak and upright tripod posture are classic red flags for acute epiglottitis. Airway protection takes priority; avoid unnecessary agitation or pharyngeal examination in an unstable child. Hib was a major historical cause, although vaccination changes epidemiology and other bacteria may be involved.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Acute bronchitis: does not usually cause drooling, tripod posture or rapidly progressive upper-airway obstruction.\n- I-b. Acute epiglottitis: correct; classic toxic child with drooling and airway compromise.\n- I-c. Acute laryngitis: usually hoarseness and less severe airway compromise.\n- I-d. Acute laryngotracheitis/croup: barking cough and stridor are typical; drooling and toxic tripod posture suggest epiglottitis instead.\n- II-a. Haemophilus influenzae: source answer; classically H. influenzae type b.\n- II-b. RSV: can cause bronchiolitis/lower respiratory infection, not the classic epiglottitis pathogen.\n- II-c. Parainfluenza: common cause of croup.\n- II-d. Staphylococcus aureus: can cause epiglottitis but is not the classic textbook answer.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSevere sore throat, drooling, toxic appearance, rapidly progressive respiratory distress, inability to speak and upright tripod posture are classic red flags for acute epiglottitis.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q32 — Acute epiglottitis: presentation and likely pathogen","aoSource":"PDF p. 46 · Printed p. 45 · Original question number: Q32","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q32-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 5-year-old boy developed severe sore throat, drippling of saliva. History of fever & increasing breathing difficulty over 8 hours. He looks toxic & unable to speak. Was sitting immobile upright with an open mouth to optimize the airway. Her airway was maintained with endotracheal tube: What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":["Acute bronchitis.","Acute epiglottitis.","Acute laryngitis.","Acute laryngotracheitis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Severe sore throat, drooling, toxic appearance, rapidly progressive respiratory distress, inability to speak and upright tripod posture are classic red flags for acute epiglottitis.","more":"Explanation:\nSevere sore throat, drooling, toxic appearance, rapidly progressive respiratory distress, inability to speak and upright tripod posture are classic red flags for acute epiglottitis. Airway protection takes priority; avoid unnecessary agitation or pharyngeal examination in an unstable child. Hib was a major historical cause, although vaccination changes epidemiology and other bacteria may be involved.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Acute bronchitis: does not usually cause drooling, tripod posture or rapidly progressive upper-airway obstruction.\n- I-b. Acute epiglottitis: correct; classic toxic child with drooling and airway compromise.\n- I-c. Acute laryngitis: usually hoarseness and less severe airway compromise.\n- I-d. Acute laryngotracheitis/croup: barking cough and stridor are typical; drooling and toxic tripod posture suggest epiglottitis instead.\n- II-a. Haemophilus influenzae: source answer; classically H. influenzae type b.\n- II-b. RSV: can cause bronchiolitis/lower respiratory infection, not the classic epiglottitis pathogen.\n- II-c. Parainfluenza: common cause of croup.\n- II-d. Staphylococcus aureus: can cause epiglottitis but is not the classic textbook answer.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSevere sore throat, drooling, toxic appearance, rapidly progressive respiratory distress, inability to speak and upright tripod posture are classic red flags for acute epiglottitis.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q32 — Acute epiglottitis: presentation and likely pathogen","aoSource":"PDF p. 46 · Printed p. 45 · Original question number: Q32","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q33","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"The DINAMAP is a device used to measure:","o":["Blood pressure.","Oxygen saturation.","FiO2.","Cardiac output."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"DINAMAP is a brand/type of automated oscillometric monitor used to obtain non-invasive blood pressure, and in some models pulse rate and other derived readings.","more":"Explanation:\nDINAMAP is a brand/type of automated oscillometric monitor used to obtain non-invasive blood pressure, and in some models pulse rate and other derived readings. It is not a pulse oximeter, oxygen analyzer or cardiac output monitor.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Blood pressure: correct.\n- b. Oxygen saturation: measured by pulse oximetry.\n- c. FiO2: measured using an oxygen analyzer.\n- d. Cardiac output: requires specialized hemodynamic measurement/estimation, not a standard DINAMAP cuff.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDINAMAP is a brand/type of automated oscillometric monitor used to obtain non-invasive blood pressure, and in some models pulse rate and other derived readings.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q33 — DINAMAP: device function","aoSource":"PDF p. 47 · Printed p. 46 · Original question number: Q33","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q34","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In shock all are true except:","o":["CVP in ↑ in cardiogenic shock.","BP is decreased in all stages.","Severe dehydration may cause hypovolemic shock.","Tension pneumothorax may cause obstructive shock.","CVP is low in hypovolemic shock."],"ol":["A","B","C","D","E"],"k":1,"ex":"Children can compensate for shock through tachycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction, maintaining blood pressure until late decompensation.","more":"Explanation:\nChildren can compensate for shock through tachycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction, maintaining blood pressure until late decompensation. CVP tends to be elevated in cardiogenic shock and low in hypovolemic shock. Tension pneumothorax can cause obstructive shock by impairing venous return.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True in typical cardiogenic shock: elevated CVP from impaired forward flow and venous congestion.\n- b. Correct exception: BP is not necessarily low in early compensated shock.\n- c. True: severe dehydration causes hypovolemic shock.\n- d. True: tension pneumothorax can obstruct venous return and produce obstructive shock.\n- e. True: CVP is generally low in hypovolemia.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nChildren can compensate for shock through tachycardia and peripheral vasoconstriction, maintaining blood pressure until late decompensation.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q34 — Shock: blood pressure may be preserved in early shock","aoSource":"PDF p. 47 · Printed p. 46 · Original question number: Q34","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q35","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In shock, all are true except:","o":["CVP (central venous pressure) is elevated in cardiogenic shock.","Blood pressure is not decreased in early (grade 1) shock.","Cardiac output is decreased in distributive (kinetic) shock.","CVP is low in hypovolemic shock."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Distributive shock is characterized primarily by reduced systemic vascular resistance and maldistribution of blood flow.","more":"Explanation:\nDistributive shock is characterized primarily by reduced systemic vascular resistance and maldistribution of blood flow. In early septic shock, cardiac output may be high; in late septic shock or associated myocardial dysfunction it may be low. Therefore the statement that cardiac output is always decreased is not correct.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True in classic cardiogenic shock: CVP commonly rises.\n- b. True: blood pressure can remain normal during compensated early shock.\n- c. Correct exception: cardiac output is not invariably decreased in distributive shock; it may be increased early.\n- d. True: CVP is generally low in hypovolemic shock.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDistributive shock is characterized primarily by reduced systemic vascular resistance and maldistribution of blood flow.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q35 — Distributive shock: cardiac output statement","aoSource":"PDF p. 47 · Printed p. 46 · Original question number: Q35","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q36","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Manifestation of hypovolemic shock include all except:","o":["Pallor & cold extremities.","Tachycardia.","High CVP.","Increased SVR."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Hypovolemia lowers venous return and cardiac preload, reducing CVP and stroke volume.","more":"Explanation:\nHypovolemia lowers venous return and cardiac preload, reducing CVP and stroke volume. The sympathetic response produces tachycardia, peripheral vasoconstriction and increased systemic vascular resistance, with cool pale extremities.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Pallor and cold extremities: typical peripheral vasoconstriction.\n- b. Tachycardia: common compensatory response.\n- c. High CVP: correct exception; CVP is usually low in hypovolemia.\n- d. Increased SVR: compensatory response to reduced circulating volume.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypovolemia lowers venous return and cardiac preload, reducing CVP and stroke volume.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q36 — Hypovolemic shock: high CVP as exception","aoSource":"PDF p. 47 · Printed p. 46 · Original question number: Q36","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q37-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 10-year-old boy presented with impaired level of consciousness of 12 hours duration. The patient could be aroused for only short periods during which he responded to simple verbal and motor commands. The mother says he was perfectly normal when he went to play football with his neighbors. On arrival, his Hb was 7 gm/dl, temperature 37.2°C, HR 55/minute, BP 130/80. The child is described to have:","o":["Stupor.","Delirium.","Light coma.","Deep coma."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The child has markedly impaired consciousness with brief arousability, compatible with stupor in the traditional descriptive scheme.","more":"Explanation:\nThe child has markedly impaired consciousness with brief arousability, compatible with stupor in the traditional descriptive scheme. Sudden onset and relative bradycardia with elevated blood pressure raise concern for intracranial pathology/raised intracranial pressure. However, the data do not prove hemorrhage. Urgent ABC stabilization, bedside glucose, neurological examination, CBC confirmation, coagulation studies and urgent neuroimaging are required according to clinical stability.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Stupor: source answer; brief arousal with stimulation and transient response fits the description.\n- I-b. Delirium: typically involves disturbed attention, awareness and cognition rather than profound reduced arousal.\n- I-c. Light coma: may overlap with stupor in older scales; definitions are not standardized.\n- I-d. Deep coma: less compatible because the child can be aroused briefly.\n- II-a. DKA: no polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration or Kussmaul pattern is provided.\n- II-b. Intracranial hemorrhage: plausible and potentially urgent, but not confirmable from the limited case data.\n- II-c. Renal failure: no renal history/laboratory data to support it.\n- II-d. Acute hepatic failure: no jaundice, liver dysfunction or metabolic evidence supplied.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe child has markedly impaired consciousness with brief arousability, compatible with stupor in the traditional descriptive scheme.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q37 — Stupor and suspected intracranial hemorrhage","aoSource":"PDF p. 47 · Printed p. 46 · Original question number: Q37","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q37-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 10-year-old boy presented with impaired level of consciousness of 12 hours duration. The patient could be aroused for only short periods during which he responded to simple verbal and motor commands. The mother says he was perfectly normal when he went to play football with his neighbors. On arrival, his Hb was 7 gm/dl, temperature 37.2°C, HR 55/minute, BP 130/80. The child is described to have:","o":["Stupor.","Delirium.","Light coma.","Deep coma."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The child has markedly impaired consciousness with brief arousability, compatible with stupor in the traditional descriptive scheme.","more":"Explanation:\nThe child has markedly impaired consciousness with brief arousability, compatible with stupor in the traditional descriptive scheme. Sudden onset and relative bradycardia with elevated blood pressure raise concern for intracranial pathology/raised intracranial pressure. However, the data do not prove hemorrhage. Urgent ABC stabilization, bedside glucose, neurological examination, CBC confirmation, coagulation studies and urgent neuroimaging are required according to clinical stability.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Stupor: source answer; brief arousal with stimulation and transient response fits the description.\n- I-b. Delirium: typically involves disturbed attention, awareness and cognition rather than profound reduced arousal.\n- I-c. Light coma: may overlap with stupor in older scales; definitions are not standardized.\n- I-d. Deep coma: less compatible because the child can be aroused briefly.\n- II-a. DKA: no polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration or Kussmaul pattern is provided.\n- II-b. Intracranial hemorrhage: plausible and potentially urgent, but not confirmable from the limited case data.\n- II-c. Renal failure: no renal history/laboratory data to support it.\n- II-d. Acute hepatic failure: no jaundice, liver dysfunction or metabolic evidence supplied.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe child has markedly impaired consciousness with brief arousability, compatible with stupor in the traditional descriptive scheme.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q37 — Stupor and suspected intracranial hemorrhage","aoSource":"PDF p. 47 · Printed p. 46 · Original question number: Q37","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q38-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 10-year-old girl presented to ER with abdominal pains and recurrent vomiting in the last few days. On examination, she was afebrile (temperature 36.8°C), moderately dehydrated with impaired LOC (level of consciousness). The mother said that her daughter was drinking too much water and juices in the last week. Part I: The most urgent lab tests you need to request is:","o":["Blood urea and serum creatinine.","Blood sugar.","Blood ammonia.","CSF examination and culture."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Polydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing.","more":"Explanation:\nPolydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing. In DKA, insulin deficiency causes ketone accumulation and high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Kussmaul breathing is deep and rapid compensatory hyperventilation that lowers PaCO2.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Urea/creatinine: useful for renal function and dehydration assessment but not the single most urgent first test.\n- I-b. Blood sugar: correct; immediate bedside glucose is essential.\n- I-c. Ammonia: considered in selected unexplained encephalopathy/liver-metabolic cases, not first here.\n- I-d. CSF: not the first step in this characteristic metabolic presentation and may be unsafe before stabilization.\n- II-a. Rapid and deep: correct; Kussmaul respiration compensates for metabolic acidosis.\n- II-b. Rapid and shallow: not the classic pattern.\n- II-c. Slow and deep: not the usual compensatory pattern.\n- II-d. Slow and shallow: would worsen hypercapnia and is not expected.\n- III-a. Metabolic acidosis: correct due to ketoacid accumulation.\n- III-b. Metabolic alkalosis: vomiting can cause alkalosis but does not explain DKA physiology.\n- III-c. Respiratory acidosis: the respiratory compensation is generally a fall in PaCO2.\n- III-d. Respiratory alkalosis: may coexist but the primary disorder is metabolic acidosis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPolydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q38 — DKA: urgent test, Kussmaul breathing and acid-base disorder","aoSource":"PDF p. 48 · Printed p. 47 · Original question number: Q38","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q38-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 10-year-old girl presented to ER with abdominal pains and recurrent vomiting in the last few days. On examination, she was afebrile (temperature 36.8°C), moderately dehydrated with impaired LOC (level of consciousness). The mother said that her daughter was drinking too much water and juices in the last week. Part II: Breathing is expected to be:","o":["Rapid and deep.","Rapid and shallow.","Slow and deep.","Slow and shallow."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Polydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing.","more":"Explanation:\nPolydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing. In DKA, insulin deficiency causes ketone accumulation and high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Kussmaul breathing is deep and rapid compensatory hyperventilation that lowers PaCO2.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Urea/creatinine: useful for renal function and dehydration assessment but not the single most urgent first test.\n- I-b. Blood sugar: correct; immediate bedside glucose is essential.\n- I-c. Ammonia: considered in selected unexplained encephalopathy/liver-metabolic cases, not first here.\n- I-d. CSF: not the first step in this characteristic metabolic presentation and may be unsafe before stabilization.\n- II-a. Rapid and deep: correct; Kussmaul respiration compensates for metabolic acidosis.\n- II-b. Rapid and shallow: not the classic pattern.\n- II-c. Slow and deep: not the usual compensatory pattern.\n- II-d. Slow and shallow: would worsen hypercapnia and is not expected.\n- III-a. Metabolic acidosis: correct due to ketoacid accumulation.\n- III-b. Metabolic alkalosis: vomiting can cause alkalosis but does not explain DKA physiology.\n- III-c. Respiratory acidosis: the respiratory compensation is generally a fall in PaCO2.\n- III-d. Respiratory alkalosis: may coexist but the primary disorder is metabolic acidosis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPolydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q38 — DKA: urgent test, Kussmaul breathing and acid-base disorder","aoSource":"PDF p. 48 · Printed p. 47 · Original question number: Q38","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q38-III","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 10-year-old girl presented to ER with abdominal pains and recurrent vomiting in the last few days. On examination, she was afebrile (temperature 36.8°C), moderately dehydrated with impaired LOC (level of consciousness). The mother said that her daughter was drinking too much water and juices in the last week. Part III: The child is expected to have:","o":["Metabolic acidosis.","Metabolic alkalosis.","Respiratory acidosis.","Respiratory alkalosis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Polydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing.","more":"Explanation:\nPolydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing. In DKA, insulin deficiency causes ketone accumulation and high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis. Kussmaul breathing is deep and rapid compensatory hyperventilation that lowers PaCO2.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Urea/creatinine: useful for renal function and dehydration assessment but not the single most urgent first test.\n- I-b. Blood sugar: correct; immediate bedside glucose is essential.\n- I-c. Ammonia: considered in selected unexplained encephalopathy/liver-metabolic cases, not first here.\n- I-d. CSF: not the first step in this characteristic metabolic presentation and may be unsafe before stabilization.\n- II-a. Rapid and deep: correct; Kussmaul respiration compensates for metabolic acidosis.\n- II-b. Rapid and shallow: not the classic pattern.\n- II-c. Slow and deep: not the usual compensatory pattern.\n- II-d. Slow and shallow: would worsen hypercapnia and is not expected.\n- III-a. Metabolic acidosis: correct due to ketoacid accumulation.\n- III-b. Metabolic alkalosis: vomiting can cause alkalosis but does not explain DKA physiology.\n- III-c. Respiratory acidosis: the respiratory compensation is generally a fall in PaCO2.\n- III-d. Respiratory alkalosis: may coexist but the primary disorder is metabolic acidosis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPolydipsia, vomiting, dehydration, abdominal pain and altered consciousness should prompt immediate glucose and ketone testing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q38 — DKA: urgent test, Kussmaul breathing and acid-base disorder","aoSource":"PDF p. 48 · Printed p. 47 · Original question number: Q38","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"III"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q39","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"A child with a Glasgow coma score of 15 is expected to be:","o":["Fully conscious.","In grade I coma (stupor).","In grade II coma (light coma).","In grade III coma (deep coma)."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The Glasgow Coma Scale totals eye (1–4), verbal (1–5) and motor (1–6) responses, for a maximum of 15.","more":"Explanation:\nThe Glasgow Coma Scale totals eye (1–4), verbal (1–5) and motor (1–6) responses, for a maximum of 15. A score of 15 generally indicates normal wakefulness and appropriate responses; interpretation should include age-appropriate verbal assessment in young children.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Fully conscious: correct for a typical patient with GCS 15.\n- b. Grade I coma/stupor: inconsistent with maximum GCS.\n- c. Grade II/light coma: inconsistent with maximum GCS.\n- d. Grade III/deep coma: inconsistent with maximum GCS.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe Glasgow Coma Scale totals eye (1–4), verbal (1–5) and motor (1–6) responses, for a maximum of 15.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q39 — Glasgow Coma Scale score of 15","aoSource":"PDF p. 48 · Printed p. 47 · Original question number: Q39","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q4","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In type 1 respiratory failure all of the following are true except:","o":["The basic defect is in oxygenation.","PaCO2 is always elevated.","Acute metabolic acidosis is a common acid base disturbance.","Pneumonia is among its leading causes."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Type 1 (hypoxemic) respiratory failure is characterized by low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2, commonly due to ventilation-perfusion mismatch or shunt (for example pneumonia).","more":"Explanation:\nType 1 (hypoxemic) respiratory failure is characterized by low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2, commonly due to ventilation-perfusion mismatch or shunt (for example pneumonia). Type 2 (hypercapnic) failure reflects inadequate alveolar ventilation with raised PaCO2. Acid–base status depends on the underlying cause and duration; an acute metabolic acidosis is not a defining feature of type 1 failure.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True: impaired oxygenation is the central defect.\n- b. False: PaCO2 may be normal or low; elevation is characteristic of type 2 failure.\n- c. Unclear/overgeneralized: metabolic acidosis may coexist in sepsis/shock but is not the usual defining disturbance of type 1 failure.\n- d. True: pneumonia is a major cause of hypoxemic respiratory failure.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nType 1 = hypoxemia; type 2 = hypercapnia due to alveolar hypoventilation.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q4 — Type 1 respiratory failure: oxygenation and acid-base pattern","aoSource":"PDF p. 41 · Printed p. 40 · Original question number: Q4","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q40","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Which of the following is likely to cause afterload heart failure:","o":["Severe tachycardia.","Rheumatic myocarditis.","Systemic hypertension.","Myocardial infarction."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Afterload is the ventricular wall stress/resistance faced during ejection.","more":"Explanation:\nAfterload is the ventricular wall stress/resistance faced during ejection. Systemic hypertension increases left ventricular afterload and chronic pressure overload can cause hypertrophy and eventual ventricular dysfunction. Myocarditis and myocardial infarction primarily impair myocardial contractility; severe tachycardia can cause a different mechanism of failure.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Severe tachycardia: may cause tachycardia-induced cardiomyopathy, not classically afterload-driven failure.\n- b. Rheumatic myocarditis: causes myocardial inflammation/contractile dysfunction.\n- c. Systemic hypertension: correct; raises systemic vascular resistance and LV afterload.\n- d. Myocardial infarction: causes ischemic loss of contractile myocardium.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAfterload is the ventricular wall stress/resistance faced during ejection.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q40 — Systemic hypertension and afterload-related heart failure","aoSource":"PDF p. 48 · Printed p. 47 · Original question number: Q40","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q41","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Investigation","q":"Which lab finding is considered abnormal:","o":["Serum K* of 4 mEq/L.","Serum creatinine of 2 mg/dl.","Blood urea of 25 mg/dl.","PH of blood 7.4."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"A serum creatinine of 2 mg/dL is markedly abnormal for most children and suggests impaired renal filtration, depending on age, baseline and muscle mass.","more":"Explanation:\nA serum creatinine of 2 mg/dL is markedly abnormal for most children and suggests impaired renal filtration, depending on age, baseline and muscle mass. Potassium 4 mEq/L and pH 7.4 are within usual reference limits; urea 25 mg/dL is within the source’s stated range.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. K 4 mEq/L: generally normal.\n- b. Creatinine 2 mg/dL: correct; markedly elevated for most children and above the reference range printed in the source.\n- c. Urea 25 mg/dL: within the range printed in the source (20–40 mg/dL).\n- d. pH 7.4: within the usual arterial pH range.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA serum creatinine of 2 mg/dL is markedly abnormal for most children and suggests impaired renal filtration, depending on age, baseline and muscle mass.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q41 — Abnormal laboratory finding: serum creatinine","aoSource":"PDF p. 48 · Printed p. 47 · Original question number: Q41","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q42","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Main step in the management of coma in children include all of the following except:","o":["Keep open airway.","Artificial breathing.","Cardiac decompression.","Defibrillation."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Initial management of an unconscious child follows ABCDE: ensure airway patency, assess/support breathing, assess circulation, check disability (including bedside glucose) and expose while preventing hypothermia.","more":"Explanation:\nInitial management of an unconscious child follows ABCDE: ensure airway patency, assess/support breathing, assess circulation, check disability (including bedside glucose) and expose while preventing hypothermia. CPR, chest compressions and defibrillation are indicated only when cardiac arrest/rhythm criteria are met. “Cardiac decompression” is not a standard step.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Keep airway open: correct priority in an unconscious child.\n- b. Artificial breathing: provide ventilatory support when breathing is inadequate/apneic.\n- c. Cardiac decompression: not a recognized generic coma-management step; likely source wording/OCR problem.\n- d. Defibrillation: not part of routine coma care unless a shockable rhythm/cardiac arrest is diagnosed. This is another reason the single-exception structure is unclear.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInitial management of an unconscious child follows ABCDE: ensure airway patency, assess/support breathing, assess circulation, check disability (including bedside glucose) and expose while preventing hypothermia.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q42 — Coma management: “cardiac decompression” wording","aoSource":"PDF p. 49 · Printed p. 48 · Original question number: Q42","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q43","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Main step in management of coma in children include all except:","o":["Maintain oxygenation.","Cardiac decompression.","Check blood sugar.","Keep airway open."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"For a comatose child, maintain airway and oxygenation, assess ventilation/circulation, check bedside glucose and treat reversible causes.","more":"Explanation:\nFor a comatose child, maintain airway and oxygenation, assess ventilation/circulation, check bedside glucose and treat reversible causes. Cardiac compression is used for cardiac arrest, not for coma with a pulse; “cardiac decompression” has no standard role in this algorithm.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Maintain oxygenation: correct.\n- b. Cardiac decompression: source answer; not a standard intervention for coma. If the intended phrase was “cardiac compression,” that is indicated only in cardiac arrest, not routine coma with circulation.\n- c. Check blood sugar: correct; hypoglycemia is rapidly reversible and must be assessed.\n- d. Keep airway open: correct.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nFor a comatose child, maintain airway and oxygenation, assess ventilation/circulation, check bedside glucose and treat reversible causes.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q43 — Coma management: repeated ambiguous “cardiac decompression” option","aoSource":"PDF p. 49 · Printed p. 48 · Original question number: Q43","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q44","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Definition","q":"The term status epilepticus is used when the clonic phase of a tonic clonic seizure exceeds...minutes:","o":["10.","15.","20.","30."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Historically, status epilepticus was defined as continuous seizure activity lasting more than 30 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery.","more":"Explanation:\nHistorically, status epilepticus was defined as continuous seizure activity lasting more than 30 minutes or repeated seizures without recovery. The ILAE operational framework distinguishes t1 (5 minutes for tonic-clonic seizures, when emergency treatment should begin) from t2 (30 minutes, when risk of long-term consequences increases). Modern emergency guidelines initiate treatment at 5 minutes, not 30 minutes.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. 10 min: not the conventional 30-minute historical definition and too long for current treatment threshold.\n- b. 15 min: neither the traditional 30-minute threshold nor current 5-minute trigger.\n- c. 20 min: neither threshold.\n- d. 30 min: correct according to the older conventional definition used by the source, but it must be annotated as outdated for immediate treatment.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCurrent emergency action: treat generalized convulsive seizure at 5 minutes; 30 minutes is the later ILAE t2 point, not a reason to wait.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q44 — Status epilepticus: traditional 30-minute definition versus current treatment threshold","aoSource":"PDF p. 49 · Printed p. 48 · Original question number: Q44","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q45","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"The acidotic breathing is:","o":["Shallow rapid.","Deep rapid.","Irregular slow.","Irregular rapid."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Metabolic acidosis stimulates increased alveolar ventilation to reduce PaCO2 and partially compensate for the fall in bicarbonate/pH.","more":"Explanation:\nMetabolic acidosis stimulates increased alveolar ventilation to reduce PaCO2 and partially compensate for the fall in bicarbonate/pH. Kussmaul respiration is characteristically deep, labored and rapid, and is classically associated with severe DKA.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Shallow rapid: may occur in respiratory distress but is not the classic Kussmaul pattern.\n- b. Deep rapid: correct.\n- c. Irregular slow: opposite of expected compensation.\n- d. Irregular rapid: not the classic pattern.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nMetabolic acidosis stimulates increased alveolar ventilation to reduce PaCO2 and partially compensate for the fall in bicarbonate/pH.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q45 — Acidotic (Kussmaul) breathing pattern","aoSource":"PDF p. 49 · Printed p. 48 · Original question number: Q45","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q46","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"In pediatric emergency situations:","o":["Primary survey aims at discovering the failing system.","Hypoxia is a relatively common event.","Infants have relatively small tongue.","Specific treatment is the first priority."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Pediatric emergency assessment prioritizes rapid identification and treatment of life-threatening problems using ABCDE.","more":"Explanation:\nPediatric emergency assessment prioritizes rapid identification and treatment of life-threatening problems using ABCDE. Hypoxia is common in critically ill children and may deteriorate rapidly. Infants have relatively large tongues and a more anterior/superior larynx, contributing to airway-management differences. Definitive etiologic treatment follows immediate stabilization.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Primary survey aims at discovering the failing system: true in broad terms; it identifies life-threatening airway, breathing, circulation, disability and exposure problems.\n- b. Hypoxia is relatively common: true.\n- c. Infants have relatively small tongue: false; the tongue is proportionally large.\n- d. Specific treatment is the first priority: false; immediate resuscitation/stabilization comes first.\nThere are at least two true options, so b is not uniquely correct.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPediatric emergency assessment prioritizes rapid identification and treatment of life-threatening problems using ABCDE.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q46 — Pediatric emergency primary survey and hypoxia","aoSource":"PDF p. 49 · Printed p. 48 · Original question number: Q46","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q47","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Equipment","q":"Advantages of flow-inflating (anesthesia) bags include all except:","o":["Easy to use.","Stiff lung can be recognized.","Any leak is determined","Delivers l00% oxygen at all times."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Flow-inflating bags require a continuous gas source and operator skill to maintain inflation and an effective seal.","more":"Explanation:\nFlow-inflating bags require a continuous gas source and operator skill to maintain inflation and an effective seal. They can provide tactile feedback about lung compliance and reveal leaks, but delivered oxygen concentration depends on the source gas and equipment setup; it should not be assumed to be 100% in every circumstance.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Easy to use: often considered false; flow-inflating bags require more technique than self-inflating bags.\n- b. Stiff lung can be recognized: generally true because compliance/pressure feedback is felt by the operator.\n- c. Any leak is determined: a leak may be apparent because the bag will not remain inflated or pressure cannot be maintained.\n- d. Delivers 100% oxygen at all times: overgeneralized and not guaranteed in all setups; FiO2 depends on source and circuit.\nAt least a and d can be considered false as written.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nFlow-inflating bags require a continuous gas source and operator skill to maintain inflation and an effective seal.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q47 — Flow-inflating bag: advantages","aoSource":"PDF p. 49 · Printed p. 48 · Original question number: Q47","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q48","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Common findings in infants with congestive heart failure include all the following except:","o":["Tachypnea.","Tachycardia.","Excessive sweating.","Splenomegaly."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Infant heart failure commonly presents with tachypnea, tachycardia, feeding difficulty, diaphoresis during feeds, poor growth and hepatomegaly.","more":"Explanation:\nInfant heart failure commonly presents with tachypnea, tachycardia, feeding difficulty, diaphoresis during feeds, poor growth and hepatomegaly. Splenomegaly is not a typical defining sign of uncomplicated pediatric congestive heart failure.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Tachypnea: common due to pulmonary congestion/increased work of breathing.\n- b. Tachycardia: common compensatory response.\n- c. Excessive sweating: common, particularly during feeding.\n- d. Splenomegaly: correct exception; hepatomegaly is more typical.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInfant heart failure commonly presents with tachypnea, tachycardia, feeding difficulty, diaphoresis during feeds, poor growth and hepatomegaly.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q48 — Infant congestive heart failure: finding that is not common","aoSource":"PDF p. 49 · Printed p. 48 · Original question number: Q48","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q49","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Epidemiology","q":"The most common cause of pediatric shock is:","o":["Acute gastroenteritis.","Diabetic ketoacidosis.","Rheumatic heart disease.","Air embolism."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Acute gastroenteritis can cause severe dehydration and hypovolemic shock, particularly in settings where diarrheal disease is prevalent.","more":"Explanation:\nAcute gastroenteritis can cause severe dehydration and hypovolemic shock, particularly in settings where diarrheal disease is prevalent. However, pediatric shock may be hypovolemic, distributive/septic, cardiogenic or obstructive; the most common cause varies by region, age and clinical environment. Therefore the source answer should be read as context-specific rather than universal.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Acute gastroenteritis: can be a leading cause of hypovolemic shock in children in diarrheal-disease settings; likely intended answer.\n- b. DKA: can cause severe dehydration and shock but is not universally the most common cause.\n- c. Rheumatic heart disease: may cause cardiogenic failure but is not the commonest overall cause.\n- d. Air embolism: rare and not a common cause of pediatric shock.\nThe epidemiologic superlative cannot be verified without population/setting context.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAcute gastroenteritis can cause severe dehydration and hypovolemic shock, particularly in settings where diarrheal disease is prevalent.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q49 — Most common cause of pediatric shock","aoSource":"PDF p. 50 · Printed p. 49 · Original question number: Q49","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q5","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Differential Diagnosis","q":"Possible causes of acute respiratory failure include:","o":["Cerebral palsy.","Drug intoxication.","Acute bronchitis.","DKA."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Drug intoxication (especially opioid or sedative toxicity) can suppress central respiratory drive, causing hypoventilation, CO2 retention and type 2 respiratory failure.","more":"Explanation:\nDrug intoxication (especially opioid or sedative toxicity) can suppress central respiratory drive, causing hypoventilation, CO2 retention and type 2 respiratory failure. Cerebral palsy may predispose to chronic respiratory impairment or aspiration; acute bronchitis is not usually a cause of severe respiratory failure in an otherwise healthy child; DKA causes compensatory deep breathing rather than primary ventilatory failure.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Cerebral palsy: may cause chronic restrictive/neuromuscular problems or aspiration in severe disease, but is not the clearest acute cause here.\n- b. Drug intoxication: correct; respiratory-center depression can produce acute hypoventilation and hypercapnia.\n- c. Acute bronchitis: usually not severe enough to cause acute respiratory failure, although severe lower-airway disease can do so in selected patients.\n- d. DKA: causes Kussmaul respiration as compensation for metabolic acidosis; it is not itself a typical primary cause of respiratory failure.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDrug intoxication (especially opioid or sedative toxicity) can suppress central respiratory drive, causing hypoventilation, CO2 retention and type 2 respiratory failure.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q5 — Possible causes of acute respiratory failure","aoSource":"PDF p. 42 · Printed p. 41 · Original question number: Q5","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q50","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"Congestive heart failure in infants rarely causes:","o":["Feeding difficulty.","Pedal edema.","Hepatomegaly.","Tachycardia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Infants with heart failure often present with tachypnea, tachycardia, feeding intolerance/diaphoresis, poor weight gain and hepatomegaly.","more":"Explanation:\nInfants with heart failure often present with tachypnea, tachycardia, feeding intolerance/diaphoresis, poor weight gain and hepatomegaly. Peripheral dependent edema is less common in infants than in adults and is usually a later/severe finding.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Feeding difficulty: common due to increased work and energy expenditure.\n- b. Pedal edema: correct; relatively uncommon in infants.\n- c. Hepatomegaly: common sign of systemic venous congestion.\n- d. Tachycardia: common compensatory sign.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInfants with heart failure often present with tachypnea, tachycardia, feeding intolerance/diaphoresis, poor weight gain and hepatomegaly.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q50 — Infant heart failure: pedal edema is uncommon","aoSource":"PDF p. 50 · Printed p. 49 · Original question number: Q50","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-MAY2018-Q51","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Epidemiology / Clinical Features","q":"In isotonic dehydration","o":["It represents 75% of cases of dehydration","The tongue is moist","The turgor is normal","Coma is a typical CNS finding"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Isotonic dehydration has historically been described as the most common type in children in many settings.","more":"Explanation:\nIsotonic dehydration has historically been described as the most common type in children in many settings. Clinical signs depend on severity; tongue dryness, reduced skin turgor and altered consciousness appear with worsening dehydration.\n\nOptions analysis:\na: Source key; isotonic dehydration is commonly the predominant category in classic teaching. b: A moist tongue is not a reliable expected feature in significant dehydration. c: Skin turgor may be reduced. d: Coma is a severe late feature, not typical.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIsotonic dehydration has historically been described as the most common type in children in many settings.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q51 — Frequency of isotonic dehydration","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 173 · printed p. 172 · original Q51 · answer continues on PDF p. 173","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q51","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Physiology","q":"Important liver function includes:","o":["Maintenance of blood glucose concentration.","Excretion of nitrogen waste as ammonia.","Control of insulin release.","Production of gamma globulin."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The liver buffers blood glucose by storing glucose as glycogen after meals, releasing glucose through glycogenolysis during fasting, and producing glucose by gluconeogenesis.","more":"Explanation:\nThe liver buffers blood glucose by storing glucose as glycogen after meals, releasing glucose through glycogenolysis during fasting, and producing glucose by gluconeogenesis. It detoxifies ammonia by converting it to urea; it does not excrete nitrogen waste as ammonia. Immunoglobulin gamma globulins are produced by plasma cells, not hepatocytes.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Maintenance of blood glucose: correct; liver glycogen metabolism and gluconeogenesis are central.\n- b. Excretion of nitrogen waste as ammonia: incorrect; ammonia is detoxified mainly through conversion to urea.\n- c. Control of insulin release: pancreatic beta cells regulate insulin secretion; the liver responds to insulin but does not control its release.\n- d. Production of gamma globulin: immunoglobulins are produced by plasma cells, not liver cells.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe liver buffers blood glucose by storing glucose as glycogen after meals, releasing glucose through glycogenolysis during fasting, and producing glucose by gluconeogenesis.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q51 — Liver function: maintenance of blood glucose","aoSource":"PDF p. 50 · Printed p. 49 · Original question number: Q51","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"","aoAltSource":"Lymph Notes in Pediatrics · Hepatology · PDF p. 129 (printed p. 128, inferred) · answer PDF page(s): 129 · original Q10"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q52","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Liver cell failure is characterized by all the following, except:","o":["Gynecomastia.","Hypoammonemia.","Encephalopathy.","Bad oral smell."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Hepatocellular failure impairs metabolism of hormones and toxins, synthesis of clotting factors and detoxification of ammonia.","more":"Explanation:\nHepatocellular failure impairs metabolism of hormones and toxins, synthesis of clotting factors and detoxification of ammonia. Hyperammonemia contributes to hepatic encephalopathy. Fetor hepaticus may produce a characteristic breath odor; gynecomastia may occur due to altered sex-hormone metabolism in chronic liver disease.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Gynecomastia: can occur in chronic liver disease due to altered hormone metabolism.\n- b. Hypoammonemia: correct exception; hyperammonemia is the expected concern in hepatic failure.\n- c. Encephalopathy: recognized manifestation of severe hepatic dysfunction.\n- d. Bad oral smell/fetor hepaticus: may occur in advanced liver disease.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHepatocellular failure impairs metabolism of hormones and toxins, synthesis of clotting factors and detoxification of ammonia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q52 — Liver cell failure: hypoammonemia as exception","aoSource":"PDF p. 50 · Printed p. 49 · Original question number: Q52","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"","aoAltSource":"Lymph Notes in Pediatrics · Hepatology · PDF p. 130 (printed p. 129, inferred) · answer PDF page(s): 130 · original Q18"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q53-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 9-month-old child is referred with pallor, vomiting and diarrhea. He became unwell 48 hours ago and has rapidly deteriorated. He has wet his nappy once in the past 24 hours. He has cool mottled peripheries and a capillary refill time of 4 seconds. Pulses are equal in all 4 limbs. He responds weakly to voice. His abdomen is soft and skin turgor is reduced. What is the most likely type of shock:","o":["Cardiogenic.","Distributive.","Hypovolemic.","Septic."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The child has acute vomiting/diarrhea, severe oliguria, cool mottled extremities, prolonged capillary refill and reduced skin turgor, strongly indicating hypovolemic shock from dehydration.","more":"Explanation:\nThe child has acute vomiting/diarrhea, severe oliguria, cool mottled extremities, prolonged capillary refill and reduced skin turgor, strongly indicating hypovolemic shock from dehydration. Management requires prompt isotonic crystalloid boluses in aliquots with reassessment after each, monitoring for response and fluid overload, and ongoing replacement of losses.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Cardiogenic: less likely; no primary myocardial/structural pump failure clues.\n- I-b. Distributive: typically vasodilatory, often warm peripheries early in septic shock; the history indicates volume loss.\n- I-c. Hypovolemic: correct due to diarrheal fluid loss and dehydration.\n- I-d. Septic: can cause shock, but the case’s predominant evidence is severe volume depletion.\n- II-a. IV fluids: correct; isotonic crystalloid resuscitation with frequent reassessment.\n- II-b. Diuretics: would worsen intravascular depletion.\n- II-c. Digoxin: not indicated for volume depletion.\n- II-d. Antibiotics: only if a bacterial infection is suspected/indicated; not a substitute for volume replacement.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe child has acute vomiting/diarrhea, severe oliguria, cool mottled extremities, prolonged capillary refill and reduced skin turgor, strongly indicating hypovolemic shock from dehydration.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q53 — Infant with diarrheal dehydration and hypovolemic shock","aoSource":"PDF p. 50 · Printed p. 49 · Original question number: Q53","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q53-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 9-month-old child is referred with pallor, vomiting and diarrhea. He became unwell 48 hours ago and has rapidly deteriorated. He has wet his nappy once in the past 24 hours. He has cool mottled peripheries and a capillary refill time of 4 seconds. Pulses are equal in all 4 limbs. He responds weakly to voice. His abdomen is soft and skin turgor is reduced. What is the most likely type of shock:","o":["Cardiogenic.","Distributive.","Hypovolemic.","Septic."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"The child has acute vomiting/diarrhea, severe oliguria, cool mottled extremities, prolonged capillary refill and reduced skin turgor, strongly indicating hypovolemic shock from dehydration.","more":"Explanation:\nThe child has acute vomiting/diarrhea, severe oliguria, cool mottled extremities, prolonged capillary refill and reduced skin turgor, strongly indicating hypovolemic shock from dehydration. Management requires prompt isotonic crystalloid boluses in aliquots with reassessment after each, monitoring for response and fluid overload, and ongoing replacement of losses.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Cardiogenic: less likely; no primary myocardial/structural pump failure clues.\n- I-b. Distributive: typically vasodilatory, often warm peripheries early in septic shock; the history indicates volume loss.\n- I-c. Hypovolemic: correct due to diarrheal fluid loss and dehydration.\n- I-d. Septic: can cause shock, but the case’s predominant evidence is severe volume depletion.\n- II-a. IV fluids: correct; isotonic crystalloid resuscitation with frequent reassessment.\n- II-b. Diuretics: would worsen intravascular depletion.\n- II-c. Digoxin: not indicated for volume depletion.\n- II-d. Antibiotics: only if a bacterial infection is suspected/indicated; not a substitute for volume replacement.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe child has acute vomiting/diarrhea, severe oliguria, cool mottled extremities, prolonged capillary refill and reduced skin turgor, strongly indicating hypovolemic shock from dehydration.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q53 — Infant with diarrheal dehydration and hypovolemic shock","aoSource":"PDF p. 50 · Printed p. 49 · Original question number: Q53","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q54","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"During therapy with oxygen, all are true except:","o":["Oxygen should be warm.","Oxygen should be humidified.","Use the least possible FiO2 that can achieve acceptable level of PaO2.","Discontinue oxygen therapy when oxygen saturation is regularly above 85%."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Oxygen should be prescribed to a target appropriate for the child’s condition, using the lowest FiO2 that achieves adequate oxygenation and avoiding unnecessary hyperoxia.","more":"Explanation:\nOxygen should be prescribed to a target appropriate for the child’s condition, using the lowest FiO2 that achieves adequate oxygenation and avoiding unnecessary hyperoxia. Oxygen is not automatically discontinued at SpO2 85%; targets are condition-specific and generally higher in children without cyanotic heart disease or special circumstances. Humidification/warming may be needed for selected high-flow or prolonged delivery systems, not universally.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Oxygen should be warm: not a universal rule for every delivery device; warming depends on delivery system and clinical indication.\n- b. Oxygen should be humidified: not universally required at every flow; humidification is used selectively.\n- c. Lowest FiO2 that achieves an acceptable PaO2: sound principle to avoid hyperoxia, with target individualized.\n- d. Discontinue at saturation above 85%: clearly unsafe as a general rule; target saturation must be individualized and is usually above this level in most children.\nThe question’s “all true except” construction is imprecise because a and b are also overgeneralized.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nOxygen should be prescribed to a target appropriate for the child’s condition, using the lowest FiO2 that achieves adequate oxygenation and avoiding unnecessary hyperoxia.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q54 — Oxygen therapy: target saturation and universal warming/humidification","aoSource":"PDF p. 51 · Printed p. 50 · Original question number: Q54","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q55-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 2-week-old infant presents to emergency department with a 1-day-history of decreased feeding and lethargy. He was born at term and the delivery was uncomplicated. On physical examination, his temperature was 39oC, his heart rate is 150 beats/minute, his respiratory rate is 60 breaths per minute, his blood pressure is 50/30 mmHg, and his extremities are cool and pale with poor pulses. Part I: The most appropriate test is:","o":["ECG and echo.","CBC and blood culture.","Lumbar puncture and CT head.","Serum electrolytes and CT chest."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Neonatal sepsis may present with poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability/fever, tachypnea, poor perfusion and hypotension.","more":"Explanation:\nNeonatal sepsis may present with poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability/fever, tachypnea, poor perfusion and hypotension. This infant has shock with fever and poor pulses, making septic shock likely. Obtain blood culture and appropriate laboratory tests promptly; stabilize airway, breathing and circulation and start empiric IV antibiotics according to neonatal sepsis protocol. Lumbar puncture is deferred until safe if unstable.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. ECG/echo: may be needed if congenital/primary cardiac disease is suspected but does not replace sepsis investigation.\n- I-b. CBC and blood culture: correct among the choices; blood culture is essential before antibiotics when this does not delay treatment.\n- I-c. LP and CT head: not the immediate first choice in a hemodynamically unstable neonate; LP may be deferred.\n- I-d. Electrolytes and CT chest: labs may be obtained, but CT chest is not the key initial investigation.\n- II-a. Septic shock: correct; fever, lethargy and poor perfusion support sepsis.\n- II-b. Hypovolemia: possible in neonates, but no fluid-loss history and fever supports infection.\n- II-c. Acute hemolysis: no jaundice/pallor-only evidence or hemolysis labs.\n- II-d. Cardiogenic shock: less likely without cardiac signs/history.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nNeonatal sepsis may present with poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability/fever, tachypnea, poor perfusion and hypotension.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q55 — Neonate with sepsis and shock: initial test and diagnosis","aoSource":"PDF p. 51 · Printed p. 50 · Original question number: Q55","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q55-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 2-week-old infant presents to emergency department with a 1-day-history of decreased feeding and lethargy. He was born at term and the delivery was uncomplicated. On physical examination, his temperature was 39oC, his heart rate is 150 beats/minute, his respiratory rate is 60 breaths per minute, his blood pressure is 50/30 mmHg, and his extremities are cool and pale with poor pulses. Part II: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Septic shock.","Hypovolemia.","Acute hemolysis.","Cardiogenic shock."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Neonatal sepsis may present with poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability/fever, tachypnea, poor perfusion and hypotension.","more":"Explanation:\nNeonatal sepsis may present with poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability/fever, tachypnea, poor perfusion and hypotension. This infant has shock with fever and poor pulses, making septic shock likely. Obtain blood culture and appropriate laboratory tests promptly; stabilize airway, breathing and circulation and start empiric IV antibiotics according to neonatal sepsis protocol. Lumbar puncture is deferred until safe if unstable.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. ECG/echo: may be needed if congenital/primary cardiac disease is suspected but does not replace sepsis investigation.\n- I-b. CBC and blood culture: correct among the choices; blood culture is essential before antibiotics when this does not delay treatment.\n- I-c. LP and CT head: not the immediate first choice in a hemodynamically unstable neonate; LP may be deferred.\n- I-d. Electrolytes and CT chest: labs may be obtained, but CT chest is not the key initial investigation.\n- II-a. Septic shock: correct; fever, lethargy and poor perfusion support sepsis.\n- II-b. Hypovolemia: possible in neonates, but no fluid-loss history and fever supports infection.\n- II-c. Acute hemolysis: no jaundice/pallor-only evidence or hemolysis labs.\n- II-d. Cardiogenic shock: less likely without cardiac signs/history.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nNeonatal sepsis may present with poor feeding, lethargy, temperature instability/fever, tachypnea, poor perfusion and hypotension.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q55 — Neonate with sepsis and shock: initial test and diagnosis","aoSource":"PDF p. 51 · Printed p. 50 · Original question number: Q55","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q56","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Fluid of choice in hypovolemic shock is:","o":["Glucose 10%.","Half normal saline.","Sodium bicarbonate.","Ringer's lactate."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Hypovolemic shock requires isotonic intravascular volume replacement.","more":"Explanation:\nHypovolemic shock requires isotonic intravascular volume replacement. Ringer’s lactate is a balanced isotonic crystalloid; 0.9% saline is another accepted option. D10, half-normal saline and sodium bicarbonate are not appropriate initial resuscitation fluids for uncomplicated hypovolemic shock.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Glucose 10%: not a resuscitation crystalloid; may be used for hypoglycemia in specific doses.\n- b. Half normal saline: hypotonic and not appropriate for initial shock resuscitation.\n- c. Sodium bicarbonate: not a volume-replacement solution.\n- d. Ringer’s lactate: correct; isotonic balanced crystalloid.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypovolemic shock requires isotonic intravascular volume replacement.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q56 — Hypovolemic shock: crystalloid choice","aoSource":"PDF p. 51 · Printed p. 50 · Original question number: Q56","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q57","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"Usual signs of heart failure in infants include:","o":["Edema and ascites.","Hepatosplenomegaly.","Congested neck veins.","Tachycardia and hepatomegaly."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Infant heart failure commonly presents with tachycardia, tachypnea, feeding difficulty, diaphoresis, poor growth and hepatomegaly.","more":"Explanation:\nInfant heart failure commonly presents with tachycardia, tachypnea, feeding difficulty, diaphoresis, poor growth and hepatomegaly. Peripheral edema, ascites and visible neck-vein congestion are less consistent/common in infants than in older children/adults.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Edema and ascites: can occur in severe/systemic congestion but are not the most usual early infant signs.\n- b. Hepatosplenomegaly: hepatomegaly is common; splenomegaly is not a typical core sign.\n- c. Congested neck veins: difficult to assess and less commonly prominent in infants.\n- d. Tachycardia and hepatomegaly: correct classic infant findings.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInfant heart failure commonly presents with tachycardia, tachypnea, feeding difficulty, diaphoresis, poor growth and hepatomegaly.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q57 — Infant heart failure: common signs","aoSource":"PDF p. 51 · Printed p. 50 · Original question number: Q57","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q58","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Causes of metabolic acidosis include all except:","o":["Pyloric stenosis.","Severe gastroenteritis.","Diabetic ketoacidosis.","Shock."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Hypertrophic pyloric stenosis causes repeated loss of gastric hydrochloric acid through vomiting, leading to hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (often with hypokalemia).","more":"Explanation:\nHypertrophic pyloric stenosis causes repeated loss of gastric hydrochloric acid through vomiting, leading to hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (often with hypokalemia). Severe diarrhea causes bicarbonate loss; DKA causes ketoacid accumulation; shock causes lactic acidosis from tissue hypoperfusion.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Pyloric stenosis: correct exception; classically metabolic alkalosis.\n- b. Severe gastroenteritis: can cause metabolic acidosis due to bicarbonate loss and hypoperfusion.\n- c. DKA: causes high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis from ketone accumulation.\n- d. Shock: can cause lactic metabolic acidosis due to impaired tissue oxygen delivery.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypertrophic pyloric stenosis causes repeated loss of gastric hydrochloric acid through vomiting, leading to hypochloremic metabolic alkalosis (often with hypokalemia).","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q58 — Metabolic acidosis: pyloric stenosis exception","aoSource":"PDF p. 51 · Printed p. 50 · Original question number: Q58","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q59","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In metabolic acidosis, all the following are true except:","o":["PH of blood is below normal.","Breathing is rapid and shallow.","Hypoxia can be a cause.","Serum bicarbonate is decreased."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Metabolic acidosis is characterized by reduced bicarbonate and reduced pH.","more":"Explanation:\nMetabolic acidosis is characterized by reduced bicarbonate and reduced pH. The respiratory system compensates by increasing alveolar ventilation, lowering PaCO2. Hypoxia can contribute to lactic acidosis through anaerobic metabolism. In severe metabolic acidosis, breathing may be deep and rapid.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True: pH is low in primary metabolic acidosis.\n- b. Correct exception: compensation is typically increased/deep ventilation; “rapid and shallow” is not the characteristic pattern.\n- c. True: tissue hypoxia can generate lactic acid.\n- d. True: bicarbonate is reduced in primary metabolic acidosis.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nMetabolic acidosis is characterized by reduced bicarbonate and reduced pH.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q59 — Metabolic acidosis: respiratory compensation","aoSource":"PDF p. 52 · Printed p. 51 · Original question number: Q59","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q6-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old boy presented with impaired level of consciousness of 6 hours duration. The patient had no response at all to painful stimuli. The mother said he was drinking a lot of water and juices in the last 10 days and passing plenty of urine. The child is not known to be diabetic. On arrival, his oral temperature was 37.2°C, HR 115/minute, BP 80/50. Respiration was deep at a rate of 39 breaths per minute. CBC was normal apart from a slightly high hemoglobin level (17 gm/dl). Part I: The child is described to have:","o":["Stupor.","Delirium.","Light coma.","Deep coma."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"An undiagnosed child with recent polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration, hypotension, deep rapid breathing and reduced consciousness should be assessed immediately for DKA.","more":"Explanation:\nAn undiagnosed child with recent polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration, hypotension, deep rapid breathing and reduced consciousness should be assessed immediately for DKA. The deep breathing is compensatory respiratory response to metabolic acidosis (Kussmaul respiration). Bedside glucose and ketones, venous blood gas, electrolytes and urgent pediatric DKA protocol are required.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Stupor: less severe than no response to painful stimuli.\n- I-b. Delirium: acute fluctuating disturbance of attention/awareness, not unresponsive coma.\n- I-c. Light coma: would generally retain some response to pain in the older scale.\n- I-d. Deep coma: correct based on no response to pain.\n- II-a. DKA: correct; polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration and deep rapid breathing support it.\n- II-b. Intracranial hemorrhage: may cause coma but does not explain the preceding polyuria/polydipsia and Kussmaul pattern as well.\n- II-c. Hypoglycemia: can cause altered consciousness but does not fit the prolonged osmotic symptoms; must nevertheless be checked immediately in any altered child.\n- II-d. Renal failure: could cause encephalopathy/metabolic abnormalities but is less likely than DKA from this vignette.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAn undiagnosed child with recent polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration, hypotension, deep rapid breathing and reduced consciousness should be assessed immediately for DKA.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q6 — Deep coma and DKA in an undiagnosed child","aoSource":"PDF p. 42 · Printed p. 41 · Original question number: Q6","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q6-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 6-year-old boy presented with impaired level of consciousness of 6 hours duration. The patient had no response at all to painful stimuli. The mother said he was drinking a lot of water and juices in the last 10 days and passing plenty of urine. The child is not known to be diabetic. On arrival, his oral temperature was 37.2°C, HR 115/minute, BP 80/50. Respiration was deep at a rate of 39 breaths per minute. CBC was normal apart from a slightly high hemoglobin level (17 gm/dl). Part II: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Diabetic ketoacidosis.","Intracranial hemorrhage.","Hypoglycemia.","Renal failure."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"An undiagnosed child with recent polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration, hypotension, deep rapid breathing and reduced consciousness should be assessed immediately for DKA.","more":"Explanation:\nAn undiagnosed child with recent polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration, hypotension, deep rapid breathing and reduced consciousness should be assessed immediately for DKA. The deep breathing is compensatory respiratory response to metabolic acidosis (Kussmaul respiration). Bedside glucose and ketones, venous blood gas, electrolytes and urgent pediatric DKA protocol are required.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Stupor: less severe than no response to painful stimuli.\n- I-b. Delirium: acute fluctuating disturbance of attention/awareness, not unresponsive coma.\n- I-c. Light coma: would generally retain some response to pain in the older scale.\n- I-d. Deep coma: correct based on no response to pain.\n- II-a. DKA: correct; polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration and deep rapid breathing support it.\n- II-b. Intracranial hemorrhage: may cause coma but does not explain the preceding polyuria/polydipsia and Kussmaul pattern as well.\n- II-c. Hypoglycemia: can cause altered consciousness but does not fit the prolonged osmotic symptoms; must nevertheless be checked immediately in any altered child.\n- II-d. Renal failure: could cause encephalopathy/metabolic abnormalities but is less likely than DKA from this vignette.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nAn undiagnosed child with recent polyuria/polydipsia, dehydration, hypotension, deep rapid breathing and reduced consciousness should be assessed immediately for DKA.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q6 — Deep coma and DKA in an undiagnosed child","aoSource":"PDF p. 42 · Printed p. 41 · Original question number: Q6","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-MAY2018-Q60","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"Regarding hemodynamic parameters in the different types of shock all of the following is true except","o":["Cardiac output is decreased in hypovolemic shock","Central venous pressure (CVP) is decreased in hypovolemic shock","Cardiac output is decreased in cardiogenic shock","Central venous pressure (CVP) is decreased in cardiogenic shock"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"In cardiogenic shock, cardiac output is reduced and filling pressures/CVP are usually increased due to pump failure and congestion.","more":"Explanation:\nIn cardiogenic shock, cardiac output is reduced and filling pressures/CVP are usually increased due to pump failure and congestion. In hypovolaemic shock, both cardiac output and CVP are generally reduced.\n\nOptions analysis:\na: True in hypovolaemic shock. b: True; preload/CVP falls. c: True in cardiogenic shock. d: Correct “EXCEPT”; CVP is generally increased, not decreased, in cardiogenic shock.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIn cardiogenic shock, cardiac output is reduced and filling pressures/CVP are usually increased due to pump failure and congestion.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q60 — Haemodynamic profile of cardiogenic shock","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 174 · printed p. 173 · original Q60 · answer continues on PDF p. 174","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q60","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology","q":"In shock, all the following are true except:","o":["CVP is elevated in cardiogenic shock.","CVP is indicative of preload.","SVR is indicative of afterload.","CVP is elevated in hypovolemic shock."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"CVP approximates right atrial pressure and is sometimes used as a marker of right-sided filling pressure/preload, but it is a poor standalone predictor of fluid responsiveness.","more":"Explanation:\nCVP approximates right atrial pressure and is sometimes used as a marker of right-sided filling pressure/preload, but it is a poor standalone predictor of fluid responsiveness. Cardiogenic shock often raises CVP due to venous congestion, whereas hypovolemic shock usually lowers it. Systemic vascular resistance reflects the resistance to ventricular ejection (afterload).\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. True in classic cardiogenic shock: CVP is commonly elevated.\n- b. Simplified but broadly true: CVP reflects right-sided filling pressure/preload, though it is not a reliable standalone fluid-responsiveness test.\n- c. True: SVR is a systemic measure related to left ventricular afterload.\n- d. Correct exception: CVP is generally low in hypovolemic shock.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nCVP approximates right atrial pressure and is sometimes used as a marker of right-sided filling pressure/preload, but it is a poor standalone predictor of fluid responsiveness.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q60 — Shock hemodynamics: CVP in hypovolemia","aoSource":"PDF p. 52 · Printed p. 51 · Original question number: Q60","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q61-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 5-year-old girl is presenting with drowsiness and vomiting. She has no significant medical history. On examination, she appears unwell and irritable. She is clinically dehydrated and has diffuse abdominal tenderness. Her temperature is 37.8°C, respiratory rate 45, heart rate 170 and BP 90/50. Her breath smells strange. Part I: The most important investigation needed:","o":["Chest x-ray.","Blood culture.","Check urine for glucose.","Echo."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"The history suggests new-onset type 1 diabetes presenting with DKA.","more":"Explanation:\nThe history suggests new-onset type 1 diabetes presenting with DKA. Hyperglycemia exceeds renal glucose reabsorption, causing glycosuria and osmotic diuresis, which leads to dehydration and electrolyte loss. The most urgent test is immediate capillary blood glucose plus ketones and blood gas/electrolytes; urine glucose is the best listed option but should not replace these tests.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Chest X-ray: only if respiratory/chest indication; not the urgent test for suspected DKA.\n- I-b. Blood culture: if sepsis is suspected, but the case is more suggestive of DKA.\n- I-c. Urine glucose: source answer and supportive, but bedside blood glucose/ketones are preferred urgent tests.\n- I-d. Echo: not indicated as the first test.\n- II-a. Diarrhea: absent from the history.\n- II-b. Polyuria: correct; glucose-driven osmotic diuresis causes major fluid loss.\n- II-c. Anorexia: may reduce intake but does not explain the main fluid loss.\n- II-d. Tachypnea: a respiratory compensation sign, not the main cause of dehydration.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe history suggests new-onset type 1 diabetes presenting with DKA.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q61 — DKA: urine glucose and osmotic diuresis","aoSource":"PDF p. 52 · Printed p. 51 · Original question number: Q61","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q61-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 5-year-old girl is presenting with drowsiness and vomiting. She has no significant medical history. On examination, she appears unwell and irritable. She is clinically dehydrated and has diffuse abdominal tenderness. Her temperature is 37.8°C, respiratory rate 45, heart rate 170 and BP 90/50. Her breath smells strange. Part II: Dehydration in this case is mainly due to:","o":["Diarrhea.","Polyuria.","Anorexia.","Tachypnea."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"The history suggests new-onset type 1 diabetes presenting with DKA.","more":"Explanation:\nThe history suggests new-onset type 1 diabetes presenting with DKA. Hyperglycemia exceeds renal glucose reabsorption, causing glycosuria and osmotic diuresis, which leads to dehydration and electrolyte loss. The most urgent test is immediate capillary blood glucose plus ketones and blood gas/electrolytes; urine glucose is the best listed option but should not replace these tests.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Chest X-ray: only if respiratory/chest indication; not the urgent test for suspected DKA.\n- I-b. Blood culture: if sepsis is suspected, but the case is more suggestive of DKA.\n- I-c. Urine glucose: source answer and supportive, but bedside blood glucose/ketones are preferred urgent tests.\n- I-d. Echo: not indicated as the first test.\n- II-a. Diarrhea: absent from the history.\n- II-b. Polyuria: correct; glucose-driven osmotic diuresis causes major fluid loss.\n- II-c. Anorexia: may reduce intake but does not explain the main fluid loss.\n- II-d. Tachypnea: a respiratory compensation sign, not the main cause of dehydration.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nThe history suggests new-onset type 1 diabetes presenting with DKA.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q61 — DKA: urine glucose and osmotic diuresis","aoSource":"PDF p. 52 · Printed p. 51 · Original question number: Q61","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q62","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"Manifestations of diabetic ketoacidosis include all except:","o":["Sweating.","Vomiting.","Respiratory distress.","Abdominal pain."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"DKA commonly presents with polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration, vomiting, abdominal pain, altered consciousness and Kussmaul respirations.","more":"Explanation:\nDKA commonly presents with polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration, vomiting, abdominal pain, altered consciousness and Kussmaul respirations. Sweating is more classically an adrenergic symptom of hypoglycemia, although it is nonspecific and may occur for other reasons.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Sweating: correct exception in the classic syndrome; may indicate hypoglycemia or another condition.\n- b. Vomiting: common.\n- c. Respiratory distress/deep Kussmaul breathing: common in significant acidosis.\n- d. Abdominal pain: common, especially in moderate-to-severe DKA.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDKA commonly presents with polyuria, polydipsia, dehydration, vomiting, abdominal pain, altered consciousness and Kussmaul respirations.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q62 — DKA manifestations: sweating as exception","aoSource":"PDF p. 52 · Printed p. 51 · Original question number: Q62","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q7","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"In a patient with grade 3 coma (deep coma), which of the following is true:","o":["Can be aroused for short periods.","Can breathe spontaneously.","Responds to painful stimuli by withdrawal movements.","Is brain dead."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Deep coma does not automatically mean brain death.","more":"Explanation:\nDeep coma does not automatically mean brain death. A patient may be unresponsive but retain spontaneous respiratory and brainstem functions. Brain death requires a formal, jurisdiction-specific determination with prerequisites and testing; it cannot be inferred from coma grade alone.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Can be aroused for short periods: inconsistent with grade 3 deep coma.\n- b. Can breathe spontaneously: correct in the scale implied by the source.\n- c. Withdraws to pain: suggests a less profound level of impairment than the intended grade 3 description.\n- d. Is brain dead: incorrect; coma and brain death are distinct states.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDeep coma does not automatically mean brain death.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q7 — Grade 3 coma: preserved spontaneous breathing","aoSource":"PDF p. 42 · Printed p. 41 · Original question number: Q7","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-MAY2018-Q71-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Pathophysiology","q":"A 3 year old boy presented to the ER with history of severe persistent vomiting and diarrhea over the last 36 hours. On examination the child was severely dehydrated, pale, with cold extremities. The skin was mottled, heart rate 160/min and blood pressure 50/30 Part A: The child is expected to have:","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis","Respiratory alkalosis"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Severe diarrhoeal fluid loss with hypotension, tachycardia, cold mottled extremities and delayed capillary refill indicates decompensated hypovolaemic shock.","more":"Explanation:\nSevere diarrhoeal fluid loss with hypotension, tachycardia, cold mottled extremities and delayed capillary refill indicates decompensated hypovolaemic shock. Tissue hypoperfusion produces lactic metabolic acidosis; capillary refill is markedly prolonged.\n\nOptions analysis:\nA: a — Metabolic acidosis is expected from poor perfusion and bicarbonate loss in diarrhoea. B: d — Capillary refill greater than 5 seconds is consistent with severe shock.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSevere diarrhoeal fluid loss with hypotension, tachycardia, cold mottled extremities and delayed capillary refill indicates decompensated hypovolaemic shock.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q71 — Hypovolaemic shock after gastroenteritis","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 178 · printed p. 177 · original Q71 · answer continues on PDF p. 178","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-MAY2018-Q71-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Pathophysiology","q":"A 3 year old boy presented to the ER with history of severe persistent vomiting and diarrhea over the last 36 hours. On examination the child was severely dehydrated, pale, with cold extremities. The skin was mottled, heart rate 160/min and blood pressure 50/30 Part B: The capillary refill time of this child is expected to be:","o":["1 second","2 second s","3 seconds","More than 5 seconds"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Severe diarrhoeal fluid loss with hypotension, tachycardia, cold mottled extremities and delayed capillary refill indicates decompensated hypovolaemic shock.","more":"Explanation:\nSevere diarrhoeal fluid loss with hypotension, tachycardia, cold mottled extremities and delayed capillary refill indicates decompensated hypovolaemic shock. Tissue hypoperfusion produces lactic metabolic acidosis; capillary refill is markedly prolonged.\n\nOptions analysis:\nA: a — Metabolic acidosis is expected from poor perfusion and bicarbonate loss in diarrhoea. B: d — Capillary refill greater than 5 seconds is consistent with severe shock.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nSevere diarrhoeal fluid loss with hypotension, tachycardia, cold mottled extremities and delayed capillary refill indicates decompensated hypovolaemic shock.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q71 — Hypovolaemic shock after gastroenteritis","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 178 · printed p. 177 · original Q71 · answer continues on PDF p. 178","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q8","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other","q":"All are signs of poor peripheral perfusion, except:","o":["Peripheral cyanosis.","Increased core-peripheral temperature difference > 2°C.","Capillary refill of 4 seconds.","Skin mottling.","Cold extremities."],"ol":["A","B","C","D","E"],"k":2,"ex":"Poor peripheral perfusion in shock is suggested by cool extremities, mottling, peripheral cyanosis, prolonged capillary refill and an increased central-to-peripheral temperature gradient.","more":"Explanation:\nPoor peripheral perfusion in shock is suggested by cool extremities, mottling, peripheral cyanosis, prolonged capillary refill and an increased central-to-peripheral temperature gradient. Capillary refill of 4 seconds is abnormal in a child under appropriate measurement conditions; it should not be used as the “except” answer.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- a. Peripheral cyanosis: can indicate reduced peripheral blood flow/oxygen delivery.\n- b. Increased core-peripheral temperature difference: may accompany vasoconstriction and poor peripheral perfusion.\n- c. Capillary refill of 4 seconds: also a sign of poor perfusion; therefore it cannot be the exception as written.\n- d. Skin mottling: a sign of impaired peripheral circulation.\n- e. Cold extremities: a classic sign of peripheral vasoconstriction/poor perfusion.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIn a shocked child, interpret capillary refill together with pulse quality, extremity temperature, mental status, urine output and blood pressure.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe unresolved issue is documented above.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q8 — Signs of poor peripheral perfusion: exception","aoSource":"PDF p. 42 · Printed p. 41 · Original question number: Q8","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q9-I","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 3-year-old boy presented to ER with impaired level of consciousness and shallow irregular breathing. The PaCO2 was 62 mmHg and the PaO2 was 98 mmHg at room air. There was a history of blunt trauma to the head since 3 hours. Part I: Blood gases shows that the child has:","o":["Type 1 respiratory failure.","Type 2 respiratory failure.","Metabolic alkalosis.","Metabolic acidosis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Hypercapnia with near-normal PaO2 indicates alveolar hypoventilation and type 2 respiratory failure.","more":"Explanation:\nHypercapnia with near-normal PaO2 indicates alveolar hypoventilation and type 2 respiratory failure. In a child with recent blunt head trauma and altered consciousness, intracranial hemorrhage or brain injury may depress respiratory drive. This child requires immediate airway/breathing/circulation assessment, cervical-spine precautions and urgent neuroimaging/trauma assessment.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Type 1 RF: primarily hypoxemic; PaCO2 is usually normal or low.\n- I-b. Type 2 RF: correct; PaCO2 is elevated due to inadequate alveolar ventilation.\n- I-c. Metabolic alkalosis: not indicated by the information given.\n- I-d. Metabolic acidosis: not identified from PaCO2 and PaO2 alone; pH/HCO3 are needed.\n- II-a. Intracranial hemorrhage: most likely among the options given recent head trauma and altered consciousness.\n- II-b. Drug intoxication: can cause hypoventilation, but there is a specific head-trauma history.\n- II-c. Respiratory muscle fatigue: possible cause of type 2 failure, but not the leading explanation in this vignette.\n- II-d. DKA: causes deep compensatory breathing and metabolic acidosis, not shallow hypoventilation with isolated hypercapnia.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypercapnia with near-normal PaO2 indicates alveolar hypoventilation and type 2 respiratory failure.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Type 2 respiratory failure after head trauma","aoSource":"PDF p. 42 · Printed p. 41 · Original question number: Q9","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-EMERGENCY-Q9-II","spec":"ao-emergency","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case","q":"A 3-year-old boy presented to ER with impaired level of consciousness and shallow irregular breathing. The PaCO2 was 62 mmHg and the PaO2 was 98 mmHg at room air. There was a history of blunt trauma to the head since 3 hours. Part II: The most likely cause is:","o":["Intracranial hemorrhage.","Drug intoxication.","Respiratory muscle fatigue.","Diabetic ketoacidosis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Hypercapnia with near-normal PaO2 indicates alveolar hypoventilation and type 2 respiratory failure.","more":"Explanation:\nHypercapnia with near-normal PaO2 indicates alveolar hypoventilation and type 2 respiratory failure. In a child with recent blunt head trauma and altered consciousness, intracranial hemorrhage or brain injury may depress respiratory drive. This child requires immediate airway/breathing/circulation assessment, cervical-spine precautions and urgent neuroimaging/trauma assessment.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- I-a. Type 1 RF: primarily hypoxemic; PaCO2 is usually normal or low.\n- I-b. Type 2 RF: correct; PaCO2 is elevated due to inadequate alveolar ventilation.\n- I-c. Metabolic alkalosis: not indicated by the information given.\n- I-d. Metabolic acidosis: not identified from PaCO2 and PaO2 alone; pH/HCO3 are needed.\n- II-a. Intracranial hemorrhage: most likely among the options given recent head trauma and altered consciousness.\n- II-b. Drug intoxication: can cause hypoventilation, but there is a specific head-trauma history.\n- II-c. Respiratory muscle fatigue: possible cause of type 2 failure, but not the leading explanation in this vignette.\n- II-d. DKA: causes deep compensatory breathing and metabolic acidosis, not shallow hypoventilation with isolated hypercapnia.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypercapnia with near-normal PaO2 indicates alveolar hypoventilation and type 2 respiratory failure.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q9 — Type 2 respiratory failure after head trauma","aoSource":"PDF p. 42 · Printed p. 41 · Original question number: Q9","aoSystem":"Emergency","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q1","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"The main clinical presentation of acquired hypothyroidism include all of the following except:","o":["Short stature.","Goiter.","School underachievement.","Precocious puberty."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Acquired hypothyroidism in childhood commonly presents with slowing of linear growth, fatigue, constipation, cold intolerance, weight gain, dry skin, goiter (depending on cause), and declining school performance.","more":"Explanation:\nAcquired hypothyroidism in childhood commonly presents with slowing of linear growth, fatigue, constipation, cold intolerance, weight gain, dry skin, goiter (depending on cause), and declining school performance. Growth deceleration is an important clue. A rare exception is severe primary hypothyroidism associated with ovarian cysts or abnormal pubertal signs despite delayed bone age; this does not make precocious puberty a typical feature.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Short stature —** True; reduced thyroid hormone impairs linear growth and may cause falling height velocity.  \n- **b. Goiter —** True in causes such as autoimmune thyroiditis; it is not present in every child.  \n- **c. School underachievement —** True; fatigue and impaired concentration may affect school performance.  \n- **d. Precocious puberty —** Best “except” answer for the usual presentation; rare pseudoprecocious puberty can occur in severe longstanding hypothyroidism.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nA falling height velocity may be more informative than a single short-height measurement when assessing acquired hypothyroidism.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q1 — Typical features of acquired hypothyroidism","aoSource":"PDF p. 154 · printed p. 153 · original Q1","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q10","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Treatment","q":"Which of the following is correct about type I DM? (September, 201l)","o":["Commonly starts in the 1st year of life.","Alkalosis may form the initial presentation.","Oral hypoglycaemic are not effective therapy.","Snacks are contraindicated in between meals."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Type 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, causing absolute or near-absolute insulin deficiency.","more":"Explanation:\nType 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, causing absolute or near-absolute insulin deficiency. Lifelong insulin replacement is required. Oral agents used for type 2 diabetes are not substitutes for insulin in children with type 1 diabetes. DKA, when present, causes metabolic acidosis—not alkalosis. Meal/snack plans should be individualised to insulin regimen, carbohydrate intake, activity and glucose patterns.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Commonly starts in the first year —** False; it can occur at any paediatric age, but onset is not commonly defined by infancy.  \n- **b. Alkalosis as initial presentation —** False for DKA, which produces metabolic acidosis.  \n- **c. Oral hypoglycaemics are not effective therapy —** Correct in the sense that they do not replace essential insulin therapy.  \n- **d. Snacks contraindicated —** False; snacks may be appropriate depending on regimen, activity and glucose-management plan.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nType 1 diabetes results from autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells, causing absolute or near-absolute insulin deficiency.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q10 — Oral hypoglycaemic agents do not replace insulin in type 1 diabetes","aoSource":"PDF p. 156 · printed p. 155 · original Q10","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q11","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"One of the clinical features of type I DM is: (June, 201l)","o":["Enuresis.","Diarrhea.","Weight gain.","Loss of appetite."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Hyperglycaemia causes glucosuria and osmotic diuresis, producing polyuria and polydipsia.","more":"Explanation:\nHyperglycaemia causes glucosuria and osmotic diuresis, producing polyuria and polydipsia. In a previously dry child, new bed-wetting may be an early clue to diabetes. Weight loss, fatigue and sometimes vomiting or abdominal pain may accompany presentation.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Enuresis —** Correct; increased urine volume can cause new nocturnal enuresis.  \n- **b. Diarrhea —** Not a characteristic presenting feature.  \n- **c. Weight gain —** Weight loss is more typical in untreated type 1 diabetes.  \n- **d. Loss of appetite —** May occur when the child is unwell, but it is not the characteristic clue intended here; polyuria/polydipsia and weight loss are more typical.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nNew-onset enuresis plus thirst, polyuria or weight loss warrants immediate glucose testing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q11 — Enuresis as a presenting feature of type 1 diabetes","aoSource":"PDF p. 156 · printed p. 155 · original Q11","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q12","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Aetiology","q":"The commonest cause of precocious puberty in females is:","o":["Premature onset of normal puberty.","Androgen secreting suprarenal tumor.","Iatrogenic.","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"In girls with central precocious puberty, no structural cause is identified in many cases and the condition is termed idiopathic.","more":"Explanation:\nIn girls with central precocious puberty, no structural cause is identified in many cases and the condition is termed idiopathic. Adrenal tumours and congenital adrenal hyperplasia are causes of gonadotropin-independent androgen excess, not the commonest explanation for central pubertal progression.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Premature onset of normal puberty —** Best intended answer if this means idiopathic central activation of the normal HPG axis; wording is non-standard.  \n- **b. Androgen-secreting adrenal tumour —** Rare peripheral cause; often causes virilisation.  \n- **c. Iatrogenic —** Exogenous sex-steroid exposure can cause peripheral pubertal signs but is not the commonest cause.  \n- **d. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia —** Important cause of androgen excess and peripheral pubertal signs, but not the commonest cause of central precocious puberty in girls.\n\n**Verification note:** The source terminology has been retained. The phrase “premature onset of normal puberty” should not be copied into a modern question bank without clarification as “idiopathic central precocious puberty.”\n\nHigh-yield point:\nIn girls with central precocious puberty, no structural cause is identified in many cases and the condition is termed idiopathic.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q12 — Commonest cause of precocious puberty in girls","aoSource":"PDF p. 156 · printed p. 155 · original Q12","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q13","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"Manifestations of diabetic ketoacidosis include all the following, except:","o":["Sweating.","Vomiting.","Respiratory distress.","Abdominal pain."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"DKA commonly produces dehydration, nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain, fruity breath and Kussmaul respiration.","more":"Explanation:\nDKA commonly produces dehydration, nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain, fruity breath and Kussmaul respiration. Altered consciousness occurs in more severe cases. Sweating is not a characteristic DKA feature and should prompt consideration of hypoglycaemia or another cause.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Sweating —** Correct “except”; not a characteristic DKA manifestation.  \n- **b. Vomiting —** Common due to acidosis and metabolic stress.  \n- **c. Respiratory distress —** Deep Kussmaul breathing is a compensatory response to metabolic acidosis.  \n- **d. Abdominal pain —** Common in paediatric DKA.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDKA commonly produces dehydration, nausea/vomiting, abdominal pain, fruity breath and Kussmaul respiration.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q13 — Sweating is not a typical manifestation of DKA","aoSource":"PDF p. 156 · printed p. 155 · original Q13","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q14","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Features","q":"The clinical presenting features of DM are all except: (September, 2009)","o":["Weight gain.","Vomiting.","Polydipsia.","Enuresis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Insulin deficiency causes reduced cellular glucose utilisation, lipolysis and proteolysis, resulting in weight loss despite hyperglycaemia.","more":"Explanation:\nInsulin deficiency causes reduced cellular glucose utilisation, lipolysis and proteolysis, resulting in weight loss despite hyperglycaemia. Osmotic diuresis causes polydipsia, polyuria and sometimes enuresis. Vomiting may indicate DKA.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Weight gain —** Correct “except”; weight loss is typical at diagnosis of type 1 diabetes.  \n- **b. Vomiting —** Can occur, especially with DKA.  \n- **c. Polydipsia —** Common due to osmotic diuresis.  \n- **d. Enuresis —** May occur because of new polyuria.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInsulin deficiency causes reduced cellular glucose utilisation, lipolysis and proteolysis, resulting in weight loss despite hyperglycaemia.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q14 — Weight loss rather than weight gain in new-onset diabetes","aoSource":"PDF p. 156 · printed p. 155 · original Q14","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q15","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Treatment / Aetiology","q":"Type I DM is characterized by all the following, except: (June, 2009)","o":["Genetic susceptibility.","Mumps & chicken pox are common triggering factors.","Is treated only by insulin.","Oral hypoglycaemic drugs have therapeutic role."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":3,"ex":"Type 1 diabetes is caused by immune-mediated beta-cell destruction in genetically susceptible individuals.","more":"Explanation:\nType 1 diabetes is caused by immune-mediated beta-cell destruction in genetically susceptible individuals. Insulin replacement is essential. Viral infections have been investigated as possible environmental triggers, but naming mumps and chickenpox as common triggers is an oversimplification. Some adjunctive glucose-lowering drugs have been studied or used selectively, but insulin remains necessary.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Genetic susceptibility —** True; genetic risk, especially HLA-associated susceptibility, contributes.  \n- **b. Mumps and chickenpox are common triggers —** Overstated; viral associations are complex and these should not be presented as established common triggers.  \n- **c. Treated only by insulin —** Insulin is essential; the word “only” is too absolute if adjuncts are considered.  \n- **d. Oral hypoglycaemics have a therapeutic role —** Best “except” answer in the classical paediatric teaching context because they cannot replace insulin and are not routine primary therapy.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nInsulin is mandatory in type 1 diabetes, regardless of any adjunctive therapy.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q15 — Insulin is essential in type 1 diabetes","aoSource":"PDF p. 157 · printed p. 156 · original Q15","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q16","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Complications / Other","q":"Complications of DM in children include all the following, except: (June, 2009)","o":["Hypoglycaemic coma.","Ketoacidotic coma.","Ammonic coma.","Skin infection."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Hypoglycaemic coma and diabetic ketoacidosis are recognised severe acute complications of diabetes; skin/soft-tissue infections may also occur in people with diabetes. “Ammonic coma” is not a standard term for a recognised diabetes complication.","more":"Explanation:\nHypoglycaemic coma and diabetic ketoacidosis are recognised severe acute complications of diabetes; skin/soft-tissue infections may also occur in people with diabetes. “Ammonic coma” is not a standard term for a recognised diabetes complication. It might be an OCR corruption or an obsolete/incorrect expression, but it cannot be silently reconstructed as “hyperosmolar coma,” “hypoglycaemic coma” or “hepatic coma.” Hyperammonaemic encephalopathy is a separate clinical problem, typically related to metabolic or hepatic disease rather than a routine diabetes complication.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Hypoglycaemic coma —** A recognised life-threatening complication of severe hypoglycaemia.  \n- **b. Ketoacidotic coma —** A severe end of the DKA spectrum with impaired consciousness.  \n- **c. Ammonic coma —** Unclear/unrecognised wording; cannot be reliably appraised without the original source image.  \n- **d. Skin infection —** A recognised infection risk/complication in diabetes, though not a “coma” complication.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypoglycaemic coma and diabetic ketoacidosis are recognised severe acute complications of diabetes; skin/soft-tissue infections may also occur in people with diabetes. “Ammonic coma” is not a standard term for a recognised diabetes complication.\n\nClinical caveat:\nThe source answer c is plausible if the option is exactly “Ammonic coma,” but the wording must be checked against the PDF. No correction has been inserted.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q16 — “Ammonic coma” is not a clear recognised term","aoSource":"PDF p. 157 · printed p. 156 · original Q16","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q17","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Management","q":"Management of DKA is all the following, except:","o":["IV fluids.","IV insulin infusion.","Slow IV infusion of Na bicarbonate.","Continuous monitoring of blood sugar."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Initial treatment begins with isotonic IV fluid to restore circulation.","more":"Explanation:\nInitial treatment begins with isotonic IV fluid to restore circulation. IV insulin infusion is started after initial fluid therapy (typically at least one hour later), with potassium management and frequent glucose, electrolyte, ketone, acid–base and neurological monitoring. Routine bicarbonate does not improve outcomes and may be harmful; it is reserved for specific exceptional indications.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. IV fluids —** Essential initial treatment.  \n- **b. IV insulin infusion —** Essential to stop ketogenesis; start after initial fluids, not as an IV bolus.  \n- **c. Slow IV sodium bicarbonate —** Correct “except”; not a routine part of DKA management.  \n- **d. Continuous blood sugar monitoring —** Required, alongside monitoring of electrolytes, ketones, acid–base status and neurological state.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nDo not give bicarbonate simply because the child is acidotic. Treat the insulin deficiency and dehydration and monitor closely.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q17 — Bicarbonate is not routine DKA treatment","aoSource":"PDF p. 157 · printed p. 156 · original Q17","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q18","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Aetiology / Clinical Features","q":"Which of the following is not correct about type I DM:","o":["It's the most common type of DM.","The onset is usually acute & rapid.","It's strongly associated with obesity.","Insulin dependency is life-long (permanent)."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Type 1 diabetes is an autoimmune insulin-deficient condition and is the predominant diabetes type in many paediatric populations.","more":"Explanation:\nType 1 diabetes is an autoimmune insulin-deficient condition and is the predominant diabetes type in many paediatric populations. Presentation may be acute, sometimes with DKA. Lifelong insulin replacement is required. Obesity is more strongly associated with insulin resistance and type 2 diabetes, but children with type 1 diabetes may also have overweight/obesity.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Most common type —** Generally true in paediatric populations, though distribution varies by population.  \n- **b. Acute and rapid onset —** Often true; onset may be over days to weeks.  \n- **c. Strongly associated with obesity —** Correct “not correct”; obesity is not the defining association, although it may coexist.  \n- **d. Lifelong insulin dependency —** True; insulin is essential because beta-cell function is substantially lost.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nType 1 diabetes is an autoimmune insulin-deficient condition and is the predominant diabetes type in many paediatric populations.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q18 — Type 1 diabetes is not strongly defined by obesity","aoSource":"PDF p. 157 · printed p. 156 · original Q18","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q19","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Nutrition / Prevention","q":"Diet of DM should fulfil all the following requirements, except:","o":["Rich in fibers.","Fat needs to provide 25% of total calories.","Animal fat is more preferable than plant fat.","Carbohydrate need to provide 55-60% of total calories."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Children with diabetes need an age-appropriate, nutritionally adequate eating pattern that supports growth and glycaemic control.","more":"Explanation:\nChildren with diabetes need an age-appropriate, nutritionally adequate eating pattern that supports growth and glycaemic control. Prefer unsaturated fats from plant sources, nuts, seeds and appropriate oils over saturated-fat-rich animal sources. Carbohydrate counting or estimation is important for matching prandial insulin. Macro distribution is individualised rather than fixed for every child.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Rich in fibres —** Generally appropriate; fibre-rich foods support overall cardiometabolic health.  \n- **b. Fat 25% of calories —** A possible historical target, but not a universal fixed proportion.  \n- **c. Animal fat more preferable —** Correct “except”; plant-derived unsaturated fats are generally preferred to saturated fats.  \n- **d. Carbohydrate 55–60% —** A historical percentage range; current plans individualise carbohydrate intake and emphasise carbohydrate counting/estimation.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nQuality of carbohydrate and fat, portioning and insulin matching matter more than imposing one fixed macronutrient ratio on every child.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q19 — Dietary fat quality in diabetes","aoSource":"PDF p. 157 · printed p. 156 · original Q19","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q2","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Pathophysiology / Laboratory Interpretation","q":"Which of the following is not true about primary hypothyroidism?","o":["It is always congenital, never acquired.","May be due to aplasia of thyroid gland. T3, T4 are low.","TSH is high"],"ol":["A","B","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Primary hypothyroidism originates in the thyroid gland.","more":"Explanation:\nPrimary hypothyroidism originates in the thyroid gland. Causes include congenital thyroid dysgenesis (including agenesis/aplasia or ectopy), defects in hormone synthesis, autoimmune thyroiditis, iodine imbalance and treatment-related thyroid damage. Loss of thyroid hormone feedback increases pituitary TSH in primary disease. Central hypothyroidism is different: FT4 is low with an inappropriately low or normal TSH.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Always congenital, never acquired —** False; autoimmune thyroiditis and other acquired causes occur in childhood.  \n- **b. May be due to aplasia —** True; thyroid agenesis/aplasia is a recognised cause of congenital primary hypothyroidism.  \n- **T3, T4 are low —** Broadly true in overt untreated primary hypothyroidism; in practice FT4 is the key biochemical measure and T3 may remain normal early. The OCR line has no visible option label; none has been inserted.  \n- **d. TSH is high —** Usually true in primary hypothyroidism, with the caveat that central hypothyroidism does not follow this pattern.\n\n**Clinical caveat:** The third option is missing its option letter in the OCR. High-yield point:\nPrimary hypothyroidism originates in the thyroid gland.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q2 — Primary hypothyroidism: congenital and acquired causes","aoSource":"PDF p. 154 · printed p. 153 · original Q2","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q20","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Nutrition / Management","q":"Which of the following is not correct about dietary management of diabetic child:","o":["Carbohydrates should supply less than 40% of calories.","Proteins should cover 15-20% of total daily caloric intake.","Fat should supply 25% of the daily total caloric intake.","Food rich in fibers should be encouraged."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Contemporary paediatric diabetes nutrition therapy is individualised to support normal growth, family eating patterns, cultural needs, food preferences, activity and glycaemic goals.","more":"Explanation:\nContemporary paediatric diabetes nutrition therapy is individualised to support normal growth, family eating patterns, cultural needs, food preferences, activity and glycaemic goals. Carbohydrate intake is monitored by carbohydrate counting or estimation, but there is no single universal carbohydrate, protein and fat percentage that applies to every child. Thus, option a is not a universal recommendation; however, options b and c are also expressed as rigid percentages without context and should not be treated as mandatory targets. Fibre-rich foods are generally encouraged as part of a healthy eating pattern.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Carbohydrates <40% —** Not a universal paediatric target; the source's answer is plausible as the intended “not correct” option, but a rigid cut-off is not guideline-based.  \n- **b. Protein 15–20% —** A possible dietary distribution, but not a mandatory fixed target for every child.  \n- **c. Fat 25% —** Also a possible distribution, not a universal fixed requirement.  \n- **d. Encourage fibre-rich foods —** Broadly consistent with healthy eating guidance.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nContemporary paediatric diabetes nutrition therapy is individualised to support normal growth, family eating patterns, cultural needs, food preferences, activity and glycaemic goals.\n\nClinical caveat:\nthe question presents fixed macro percentages as if they were universal. The source answer is retained; the original PDF may clarify whether the question was copied from a specific older dietary standard.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q20 — Fixed macronutrient percentages are not universal paediatric diabetes rules","aoSource":"PDF p. 157 · printed p. 156 · original Q20","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q21","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Prevention / Lifestyle Management","q":"Which of the following is not correct about insulin-dependent diabetes mellitus:","o":["It's also called type I DM.","The affected child not to be encouraged to participate in sports.","It's a life-long disease.","The sensitivity to insulin is normal."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Exercise improves cardiovascular fitness, bone health and wellbeing.","more":"Explanation:\nExercise improves cardiovascular fitness, bone health and wellbeing. Insulin-treated children need a plan for glucose checks before/during/after activity, carbohydrate intake and insulin adjustment as appropriate, because exercise can cause hypoglycaemia during or after activity, including overnight. Diabetes is not a reason to exclude a child from sports.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Also called type I DM —** True in the traditional terminology used by the source.  \n- **b. Should not be encouraged to participate in sports —** Correct “not correct”; activity is recommended with appropriate precautions.  \n- **c. Lifelong disease —** Type 1 diabetes generally requires lifelong insulin therapy.  \n- **d. Sensitivity to insulin is normal —** Simplified; many children have no primary insulin-resistance disorder, but sensitivity varies with puberty, adiposity, illness, exercise and other factors.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nExercise improves cardiovascular fitness, bone health and wellbeing.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q21 — Physical activity should be encouraged in children with type 1 diabetes","aoSource":"PDF p. 157 · printed p. 156 · original Q21","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q22-I","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Investigation","q":"A 10-year-old girl presented to ER with abdominal pain & recurrent vomiting in the last four days. On examination, she was afebrile, moderately dehydrated with impaired consciousness . The mother said that her daughter was drinking too much water & juices in the last week. (June 2016) Part A: The most urgent lab that you need to request is:","o":["Blood urea & creatinine.","Blood sugar.","Blood ammonia.","CSF examination & culture."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Polydipsia followed by vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration and impaired consciousness is a classic warning pattern for DKA.","more":"Explanation:\nPolydipsia followed by vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration and impaired consciousness is a classic warning pattern for DKA. Obtain immediate glucose and ketone measurements, electrolytes, blood gas and renal function. Deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing is the respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis. This is an emergency requiring monitored paediatric treatment.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part A**  \n- **a. Urea and creatinine —** Useful to assess dehydration/renal perfusion but not the first screening test for the suspected cause.  \n- **b. Blood sugar —** Correct; urgent bedside glucose is the priority.  \n- **c. Blood ammonia —** Not the first test for this presentation.  \n- **d. CSF —** Not the first investigation unless CNS infection is specifically suspected.  \n\n**Part B**  \n- **a. Rapid and deep —** Correct; Kussmaul respiration compensates for metabolic acidosis.  \n- **b. Rapid and shallow —** Not the typical compensatory pattern.  \n- **c. Slow and shallow —** Would worsen CO2 retention and acidosis.  \n- **d. Slow and deep —** Not the expected pattern.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nKussmaul breathing is a clue to metabolic acidosis, not a primary respiratory disorder.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q22 — DKA: immediate glucose test and Kussmaul breathing","aoSource":"PDF p. 158 · printed p. 157 · original Q22 (A–B)","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q22-II","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Investigation","q":"A 10-year-old girl presented to ER with abdominal pain & recurrent vomiting in the last four days. On examination, she was afebrile, moderately dehydrated with impaired consciousness . The mother said that her daughter was drinking too much water & juices in the last week. (June 2016) Part B: Breathing is expected to be:","o":["Rapid & deep.","Rapid & shallow.","Slow & shallow.","Slow & deep."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Polydipsia followed by vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration and impaired consciousness is a classic warning pattern for DKA.","more":"Explanation:\nPolydipsia followed by vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration and impaired consciousness is a classic warning pattern for DKA. Obtain immediate glucose and ketone measurements, electrolytes, blood gas and renal function. Deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing is the respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis. This is an emergency requiring monitored paediatric treatment.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part A**  \n- **a. Urea and creatinine —** Useful to assess dehydration/renal perfusion but not the first screening test for the suspected cause.  \n- **b. Blood sugar —** Correct; urgent bedside glucose is the priority.  \n- **c. Blood ammonia —** Not the first test for this presentation.  \n- **d. CSF —** Not the first investigation unless CNS infection is specifically suspected.  \n\n**Part B**  \n- **a. Rapid and deep —** Correct; Kussmaul respiration compensates for metabolic acidosis.  \n- **b. Rapid and shallow —** Not the typical compensatory pattern.  \n- **c. Slow and shallow —** Would worsen CO2 retention and acidosis.  \n- **d. Slow and deep —** Not the expected pattern.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nKussmaul breathing is a clue to metabolic acidosis, not a primary respiratory disorder.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q22 — DKA: immediate glucose test and Kussmaul breathing","aoSource":"PDF p. 158 · printed p. 157 · original Q22 (A–B)","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q23-I","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Diagnosis","q":"A 9-year-old boy complaining of nocturnal enuresis of 2 months duration. He mentioned that he becomes more thirsty & drinks more fluids than before. He lost 2 Kg in the last 6 weeks. Blood urea = 20 mg/dL. Blood creatinine = 0.4 mg/dL. pH = 7.3. (June 2016) Part A: The most probable diagnosis is:","o":["Chronic renal failure.","Primary nocturnal enuresis.","Diabetes mellitus.","Diabetic ketoacidosis."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"New enuresis, polydipsia and weight loss strongly suggest diabetes mellitus, and polyuria is expected from osmotic diuresis.","more":"Explanation:\nNew enuresis, polydipsia and weight loss strongly suggest diabetes mellitus, and polyuria is expected from osmotic diuresis. However, the question gives pH 7.3 without blood glucose, bicarbonate, ketones or anion gap. DKA requires hyperglycaemia, ketosis and metabolic acidosis; pH alone is insufficient to confirm or exclude it. Therefore, the likely underlying diagnosis is diabetes mellitus, but the distinction from DKA is not secure on the supplied information.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part A**  \n- **a. Chronic renal failure —** Not supported by the history or the provided urea/creatinine values; chronicity and renal evidence are absent.  \n- **b. Primary nocturnal enuresis —** Unlikely because new thirst and weight loss are alarm features for an underlying medical cause.  \n- **c. Diabetes mellitus —** Most likely underlying diagnosis given polydipsia, weight loss and new enuresis.  \n- **d. DKA —** Possible if hyperglycaemia, ketones and metabolic acidosis are present, but those essential criteria are not supplied.  \n\n**Part B**  \n- **a. Oliguria —** Not expected in uncomplicated osmotic diuresis.  \n- **b. Polyuria —** Correct; glucosuria produces osmotic diuresis.  \n- **c. Hypervolemia —** Not expected; osmotic diuresis can cause dehydration and volume depletion.  \n- **d. Azotemia —** Not an expected defining feature; it may occur secondarily with severe dehydration/renal hypoperfusion.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nNew enuresis, polydipsia and weight loss strongly suggest diabetes mellitus, and polyuria is expected from osmotic diuresis.\n\nClinical caveat:\nRetain as because the source lacks the glucose and ketone values needed to assess DKA. Source answers c and b are preserved; B is physiologically consistent.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q23 — Diabetes mellitus versus DKA cannot be fully distinguished from the supplied data","aoSource":"PDF p. 158 · printed p. 157 · original Q23 (A–B)","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q23-II","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Clinical Case / Diagnosis","q":"A 9-year-old boy complaining of nocturnal enuresis of 2 months duration. He mentioned that he becomes more thirsty & drinks more fluids than before. He lost 2 Kg in the last 6 weeks. Blood urea = 20 mg/dL. Blood creatinine = 0.4 mg/dL. pH = 7.3. (June 2016) Part B: Which of the following is expected in this patient:","o":["Oliguria.","Polyuria.","Hypervolemia.","Azotemia."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"New enuresis, polydipsia and weight loss strongly suggest diabetes mellitus, and polyuria is expected from osmotic diuresis.","more":"Explanation:\nNew enuresis, polydipsia and weight loss strongly suggest diabetes mellitus, and polyuria is expected from osmotic diuresis. However, the question gives pH 7.3 without blood glucose, bicarbonate, ketones or anion gap. DKA requires hyperglycaemia, ketosis and metabolic acidosis; pH alone is insufficient to confirm or exclude it. Therefore, the likely underlying diagnosis is diabetes mellitus, but the distinction from DKA is not secure on the supplied information.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part A**  \n- **a. Chronic renal failure —** Not supported by the history or the provided urea/creatinine values; chronicity and renal evidence are absent.  \n- **b. Primary nocturnal enuresis —** Unlikely because new thirst and weight loss are alarm features for an underlying medical cause.  \n- **c. Diabetes mellitus —** Most likely underlying diagnosis given polydipsia, weight loss and new enuresis.  \n- **d. DKA —** Possible if hyperglycaemia, ketones and metabolic acidosis are present, but those essential criteria are not supplied.  \n\n**Part B**  \n- **a. Oliguria —** Not expected in uncomplicated osmotic diuresis.  \n- **b. Polyuria —** Correct; glucosuria produces osmotic diuresis.  \n- **c. Hypervolemia —** Not expected; osmotic diuresis can cause dehydration and volume depletion.  \n- **d. Azotemia —** Not an expected defining feature; it may occur secondarily with severe dehydration/renal hypoperfusion.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nNew enuresis, polydipsia and weight loss strongly suggest diabetes mellitus, and polyuria is expected from osmotic diuresis.\n\nClinical caveat:\nRetain as because the source lacks the glucose and ketone values needed to assess DKA. Source answers c and b are preserved; B is physiologically consistent.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q23 — Diabetes mellitus versus DKA cannot be fully distinguished from the supplied data","aoSource":"PDF p. 158 · printed p. 157 · original Q23 (A–B)","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q24-I","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Diagnosis / Differential Diagnosis","q":"A 7-year-old girl was brought by her mother to the paediatrician office complaining that her axillary & pubic hair was well developed. On examination, the pubic hair was given sexual maturity grade of 4 & breast SMR was 1. (June, 2016) Part A: The most likely diagnosis is:","o":["Premature thelarche.","Premature adrenarche.","True precocious puberty.","Pseudo precocious puberty."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Pubic/axillary hair with Tanner breast stage 1 indicates androgenic hair development without gonadal estrogen-driven breast development.","more":"Explanation:\nPubic/axillary hair with Tanner breast stage 1 indicates androgenic hair development without gonadal estrogen-driven breast development. This is compatible with premature adrenarche. However, if there is growth acceleration, the child needs assessment for androgen excess and advanced bone age. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia is an important differential; androgen-secreting adrenal or gonadal pathology may also be considered depending on virilisation and laboratory findings. The source key for part B (“hyperpituitarism”) is not a secure single-best answer from the information supplied. Growth acceleration alone does not establish pituitary hyperfunction.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part A**  \n- **a. Premature thelarche —** Refers to isolated breast development; breast SMR 1 argues against it.  \n- **b. Premature adrenarche —** Best fit for pubic/axillary hair with no breast development.  \n- **c. True precocious puberty —** Central puberty generally causes breast development in girls, with progressive HPG-axis activation.  \n- **d. Pseudo precocious puberty —** Peripheral sex-steroid excess may cause pubertal signs, but the described isolated adrenal hair pattern is more consistent with premature adrenarche.  \n\n**Part B**  \n- **a. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia —** Important cause of androgen excess, growth acceleration and advanced bone age; deserves assessment.  \n- **b. Hyperpituitarism —** Can cause accelerated linear growth in selected pituitary disorders, but does not by itself explain the androgenic pubic/axillary hair pattern; source answer is questionable.  \n- **c. Thyrotoxicosis —** May accelerate growth/maturation but does not explain isolated androgenic hair development.  \n- **d. Turner syndrome —** Typically causes short stature and gonadal insufficiency, not androgen-driven growth acceleration.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPubic/axillary hair with Tanner breast stage 1 indicates androgenic hair development without gonadal estrogen-driven breast development.\n\nClinical caveat:\nPart A is strongly supported, while part B's source key requires verification. Because the item is one parent question with a doubtful second part, the complete item remains . A clinician would assess growth velocity, bone age, DHEAS, testosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone as indicated.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q24 — Premature adrenarche and growth acceleration: second answer is questionable","aoSource":"PDF pp. 158–159 · printed pp. 157–158 · original Q24 (A–B)","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":"I"},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q24-II","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Diagnosis / Differential Diagnosis","q":"A 7-year-old girl was brought by her mother to the paediatrician office complaining that her axillary & pubic hair was well developed. On examination, the pubic hair was given sexual maturity grade of 4 & breast SMR was 1. (June, 2016)","o":["If this condition was associated with growth acceleration, one of the following should be suspected:","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia.","Hyperpituitarism.","Thyrotoxicosis.","Turner syndrome."],"ol":["B","A","B","C","D"],"k":0,"ex":"Pubic/axillary hair with Tanner breast stage 1 indicates androgenic hair development without gonadal estrogen-driven breast development.","more":"Explanation:\nPubic/axillary hair with Tanner breast stage 1 indicates androgenic hair development without gonadal estrogen-driven breast development. This is compatible with premature adrenarche. However, if there is growth acceleration, the child needs assessment for androgen excess and advanced bone age. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia is an important differential; androgen-secreting adrenal or gonadal pathology may also be considered depending on virilisation and laboratory findings. The source key for part B (“hyperpituitarism”) is not a secure single-best answer from the information supplied. Growth acceleration alone does not establish pituitary hyperfunction.\n\nOptions analysis:\n**Part A**  \n- **a. Premature thelarche —** Refers to isolated breast development; breast SMR 1 argues against it.  \n- **b. Premature adrenarche —** Best fit for pubic/axillary hair with no breast development.  \n- **c. True precocious puberty —** Central puberty generally causes breast development in girls, with progressive HPG-axis activation.  \n- **d. Pseudo precocious puberty —** Peripheral sex-steroid excess may cause pubertal signs, but the described isolated adrenal hair pattern is more consistent with premature adrenarche.  \n\n**Part B**  \n- **a. Congenital adrenal hyperplasia —** Important cause of androgen excess, growth acceleration and advanced bone age; deserves assessment.  \n- **b. Hyperpituitarism —** Can cause accelerated linear growth in selected pituitary disorders, but does not by itself explain the androgenic pubic/axillary hair pattern; source answer is questionable.  \n- **c. Thyrotoxicosis —** May accelerate growth/maturation but does not explain isolated androgenic hair development.  \n- **d. Turner syndrome —** Typically causes short stature and gonadal insufficiency, not androgen-driven growth acceleration.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nPubic/axillary hair with Tanner breast stage 1 indicates androgenic hair development without gonadal estrogen-driven breast development.\n\nClinical caveat:\nPart A is strongly supported, while part B's source key requires verification. Because the item is one parent question with a doubtful second part, the complete item remains . A clinician would assess growth velocity, bone age, DHEAS, testosterone and 17-hydroxyprogesterone as indicated.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Needs Review","aoReview":true,"aoTitle":"Q24 — Premature adrenarche and growth acceleration: second answer is questionable","aoSource":"PDF pp. 158–159 · printed pp. 157–158 · original Q24 (A–B)","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":"II"},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-Q25","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Other / Disease Classification","q":"Which of the following is not correct about insulin dependent DM:","o":["Once diagnosed, it's a life-long disease.","The affected child should be encouraged to participate in sports.","It's also called type II DM.","Sensitivity to insulin is normal."],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":2,"ex":"Type 1 diabetes is caused by beta-cell destruction and requires insulin replacement.","more":"Explanation:\nType 1 diabetes is caused by beta-cell destruction and requires insulin replacement. Type 2 diabetes is associated with insulin resistance and relative insulin deficiency, although overlap can occur in clinical practice. Exercise should be encouraged in both types, with treatment and safety plans tailored to the child.\n\nOptions analysis:\n- **a. Lifelong disease —** True for the usual course of type 1 diabetes; insulin remains essential.  \n- **b. Encourage sports —** True; physical activity is recommended with glucose monitoring and hypoglycaemia precautions.  \n- **c. Also called type II DM —** Correct “not correct”; insulin-dependent diabetes is the historical term for type 1, not type 2.  \n- **d. Sensitivity to insulin is normal —** A simplified distinction from type 2 insulin resistance; individual insulin sensitivity may vary.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nType 1 diabetes is caused by beta-cell destruction and requires insulin replacement.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q25 — Type 1 diabetes is not type 2 diabetes","aoSource":"PDF p. 159 · printed p. 158 · original Q25","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""},
{"id":"AO-ENDOCRINE-MAY2018-Q27","spec":"ao-endocrine","topic":0,"tag":"All Once","kind":"Complications","q":"Which of the following is correct about juvenile diabetes mellitus","o":["Single dose of insulin before breakfast is used for treatment","A hypoglycemia is a common acute complication","Carbohydrates should be around 25% of total caloric intake","Snacks in between meals is usually not advisable"],"ol":["A","B","C","D"],"k":1,"ex":"Hypoglycaemia is a common acute complication of insulin-treated type 1 diabetes.","more":"Explanation:\nHypoglycaemia is a common acute complication of insulin-treated type 1 diabetes. Management uses an individualised basal-bolus regimen, carbohydrate education and glucose monitoring; fixed single-dose regimens are generally inadequate.\n\nOptions analysis:\na: A single morning dose is not standard comprehensive therapy. b: Correct; hypoglycaemia is common and clinically important. c: Carbohydrate needs are individualised; 25% is not a universal target. d: Planned snacks may be useful depending on regimen and activity; blanket prohibition is incorrect.\n\nHigh-yield point:\nHypoglycaemia is a common acute complication of insulin-treated type 1 diabetes.","ao":true,"aoClass":"Accepted","aoReview":false,"aoTitle":"Q27 — Acute complications of type 1 diabetes","aoSource":"`MCQs Lymph Notes.md` · PDF p. 169 · printed p. 168 · original Q27 · answer continues on PDF p. 169","aoSystem":"Endocrine","aoPart":""}
